findings.detail:
Verified exactly as described:
```cpp
MatrixF::MatrixF(int rows, int columns)
{
    this->_rows = rows;
    this->_columns = columns;
}   // _elements (MattMath.h:723) stays empty
```
`get_element`/`set_element` (2494-2513) validate only via `row_valid`/`column_valid` (2701-2708), which test against `_rows`/`_columns`, never `_elements.size()`. So `_elements[calculate_index(row, column)]` indexes an empty vector. Every producer uses this constructor: add (2742), subtract (2761), multiply (2778), divide (2800), multiply(a,float) (2819), divide(a,float) (2833), transpose (2901), identity (2916), zero (2925), determinant's cofactor submatrix (2956), and `Matrix3x3F(float e11..e33)` (2995-3008), which delegates to MatrixF(3,3) and then performs nine out-of-bounds writes. `Matrix3x3F::rotation/scale/translation` (3010-3048) are therefore all memory-unsafe.

What keeps this from being 'critical': a repo-wide grep finds zero references to MatrixF or Matrix3x3F outside MattMath.h/.cpp. It is unreachable dead code — but it is shipped in the public engine header, so the first consumer to touch it corrupts memory.

Verified verbatim:
```cpp
void Vector2F::rotate_vector_by_ref(Vector2F& vec, float angle)
{
    float cos_angle = std::cos(angle);
    float sin_angle = std::sin(angle);
    vec.x = vec.x * cos_angle - vec.y * sin_angle;
    vec.y = vec.x * sin_angle + vec.y * cos_angle;   // reads the NEW x
}
```
Not a rotation for any angle but 0. Rotating (1,0) by 90 degrees gives (0,0). The two siblings are correct: `Vector2F::rotate(float)` (1757-1767) caches into locals, and the static `rotate_vector` (1792-1799) builds a new vector. Zero callers repo-wide (only the declaration at MattMath.h:464), which is why it has survived; `rotate_vector` is the one that is actually used (ProjectileBuilder.cpp:149,163,177,191; Weapon.cpp:150). Three reviewers reported this at severities from high to medium; graded medium because it is unreachable, but it is a public API of the engine's math library and looks like the efficient in-place variant.

Verified:
```cpp
Vector2F Vector2F::cross(const Vector2F& other) const
{
    return Vector2F(this->x * other.y, this->y * other.x);
}
```
The 2D perp-dot product is the scalar `x*b.y - y*b.x`. This returns a vector holding the two operands of that subtraction — meaningless under any interpretation; `a.cross(a)` returns a nonzero vector where the answer must be zero. It sits at MattMath.h:443 next to the (correct) `dot`, so it reads as a legitimate sibling. Zero callers, but this is exactly the primitive a separating-axis or winding test would reach for, and the collision code has no correct version of it (see the `sign` finding).

Verified:
```cpp
int MattMath::sign(const Vector2F& p1, const Vector2F& p2, const Vector2F& p3)
{
    return static_cast<int>((p1.x - p3.x) * (p2.y - p3.y) -
        (p2.x - p3.x) * (p1.y - p3.y));
}
```
This is the standard half-plane/orientation predicate, whose whole purpose is the *sign* of the determinant. `static_cast<int>` truncates toward zero, so every configuration with |determinant| < 1 — every small or thin triangle, and every near-collinear case, which is precisely what the predicate exists to resolve — returns 0. Callers written as `sign(...) < 0` / `> 0` both take the false branch. Callers comparing two sign() results for equality (the standard barycentric point-in-triangle idiom) compare magnitudes, not signs. Declared at MattMath.h:56-57, zero call sites.

Verified — the two bodies at 2385-2392 and 2393-2400 are the same six statements, differing only in the function name:
```cpp
float grey = this->r * 0.3f + this->g * 0.59f + this->b * 0.11f;
this->r = grey + amount * (this->r - grey);
this->g = grey + amount * (this->g - grey);
this->b = grey + amount * (this->b - grey);
this->clamp_colours();
```
The shared formula lerps *away* from grey as `amount` grows, i.e. it is the saturate behaviour. So `desaturate(1.0f)` — the natural spelling of 'fully desaturate' — is a no-op, and `desaturate(0.0f)` is the only value that greys the colour. Two public API names (MattMath.h:630-631) with opposite meanings doing the same thing; a caller cannot tell which one is wrong. Graded medium rather than high because both have zero callers repo-wide. The Rec.601/NTSC luma weights 0.3/0.59/0.11 are also bare literals duplicated in both bodies.

Verified:
```cpp
if (x2 >= x1 && y2 >= y1) { return RectangleF(x1, y1, x2 - x1, y2 - y1); }
else                      { return RectangleF(); }   // (0,0,0,0)
```
The disjoint case returns a rectangle at the world origin, indistinguishable from a legitimate zero-area edge touch at (0,0) — and `>=` deliberately admits edge touches, so both states really occur. The instance overload (920-923) forwards and inherits it.

The consumer does not check. CollisionTools.cpp:214-217:
```cpp
RectangleF inter = RectangleF::intersection(collider->get_bounding_box(), collidee->get_bounding_box());
Vector2F amount = { inter.width, inter.height };
...
collider->offset(opposite_direction(direction) * amount);
```
The caller established a *shape* intersection, not an AABB one, so for triangles/circles/OBBs the AABBs can genuinely be disjoint; the push-out is then silently zero and the routine reports success. `union_of` (1114-1121) has no failure case and is fine.

Verified. `from_top_left_bottom_right` (1122-1128) subtracts without any ordering check, and LevelObjectBuilder.cpp:45-51 and 88-94 feed it directly from untrusted level JSON:
```cpp
Vector2F top_left = Vector2F(json["rectangle_tlbr"]["left"].GetFloat(), json["rectangle_tlbr"]["top"].GetFloat());
Vector2F bottom_right = Vector2F(json["rectangle_tlbr"]["right"].GetFloat(), json["rectangle_tlbr"]["bottom"].GetFloat());
rectangle = RectangleF::from_top_left_bottom_right(top_left, bottom_right);
```
Swap left/right in a level file and every predicate silently returns the wrong answer rather than failing: rectangle_point_intersect (252-258) tests `x >= rect.x && x <= rect.x + width` and can never be true with negative width; contains(RectangleF) (881-887) likewise; get_right() (764-767) returns a value left of get_left(); intersection (1099) reports no overlap. The object loads, draws, and is intangible. Same exposure via the direct RectangleF(x,y,w,h) path and set_width/set_height/scale, none of which validate. Quad::is_valid (MattMath.h:940) and RectangleRotated::is_valid (1108) already establish the validity-check pattern for the other shapes; RectangleF has none.

Verified:
```cpp
const D3D11_VIEWPORT* Viewport::get_d3d_viewport_ptr() const
{
    return reinterpret_cast<const D3D11_VIEWPORT*>(this);
}
```
Strict-aliasing UB that works only because Viewport's six floats (MattMath.h:770-775) happen to be declared in the same order as D3D11_VIEWPORT's TopLeftX/TopLeftY/Width/Height/MinDepth/MaxDepth. No static_assert on size, offsets or standard-layout anywhere. Adding a member, reordering, or adding a base class silently produces garbage viewports with no diagnostic.

Graded medium rather than high because it has zero callers (grep across ArtAttack finds only the declaration at MattMath.h:790 and this definition), and the safe alternative `get_d3d_viewport()` sits three lines above at 3201-3212. So it is pure downside with no current upside.

Several candidates cited 'MattMath.cpp:4191-4192' as the site where `RectangleRotated(const Segment& center_line, float thickness)` normalises the axes of a possibly-zero-length centre line. That is the wrong constructor: 4191-4192 belongs to the four-argument constructor at 4186. I am recording the rejection here rather than dropping it, because reading the real constructor turned up a worse defect the reviewers missed:
```cpp
RectangleRotated::RectangleRotated(const Segment& center_line, float thickness)
{
    this->_center = center_line.get_center();
    this->_x_axis = center_line.get_direction();   // NOT normalised
    this->_y_axis = Vector2F::normal(this->_x_axis);
    ...
    if (!this->is_valid()) { throw std::invalid_argument("RectangleRotated is not valid"); }
```
`get_direction()` (4063-4066) returns `point_1 - point_0`, unnormalised. `axes_valid()` (4522-4538) requires `are_equal(_x_axis.length(), 1.0f, EPSILON)`. So this constructor throws for every centre line whose length is not within 1e-4 of 1.0 — i.e. essentially always. The sibling constructor at 4186-4200 calls normalize() on both axes; this one does not.

```cpp
bool MTexture::is_visible_in_viewport(const RectangleF& view) const
{
	return this->is_visible_in_viewport(view);
}
```
Unqualified lookup finds MTexture's own member (declared Mh.h:95), so this recurses on itself with identical arguments and no termination — stack overflow on the first call. There is nothing to delegate to either: IGameObject declares it pure (IGameObject.h:13), MObject and MWidget re-declare it `= 0` (Mh.h:25, 69), and TextureObject/SpriteSheetObject/DrawObject do not derive from IGameObject at all (DrawObject.h:7) so no base version exists.

Downgraded from the reviewers' "critical": it is currently unreachable. The only caller is MContainer::is_visible_in_viewport (Mh.cpp:113-123), and a repo-wide grep shows nothing calls that. So it is a landmine, not a live crash — but it detonates the moment anyone adds culling to the menu draw path.

Note the class shape invites this mistake: MTexture::set_colour (Mh.cpp:176-179) is the same forward-to-the-other-base pattern written correctly as `this->SpriteSheetObject::set_colour(colour)`.

```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++)
{
	float dist = Vector2F::dot(d, b.get_axis(i));
	if (dist > b.get_half_width(i)) dist = b.get_half_width(i);
	...
}
```
An unedited copy of Ericson's 3D routine. `MattMath::OBB` is `typedef RectangleRotated OBB` (MattMath.h:1136) and has exactly two axes: `RectangleRotated::get_axis` (MattMath.cpp:4315-4327) and `get_half_width` (MattMath.cpp:4340-4352) both `throw std::invalid_argument("Axis must be 0 or 1")` for any other index. The third iteration therefore throws unconditionally — the function can never return normally, for any input — and it escapes after `q` has already been partially written, leaving the caller's out-param meaningless.

Grep confirms zero callers outside EricsonMath.{h,cpp} (including MattMathTests), which is the only reason it has not fired. It is also new code, added with the recent RectangleRotated/OBB work, and sits in the public header (EricsonMath.h:141-142). The sibling `closest_pt_point_AABB` (lines 421-449) was correctly hand-unrolled to two axes, so the 3-iteration loop is a straight porting miss.

```cpp
if (collision_direction.x != 0.0f && collision_direction.y != 0.0f)
{
	float larger = std::max(collision_direction.x, collision_direction.y);
	direction = collision_direction / larger;
}
collider->offset(opposite_direction(direction) * amount);
```
The intent is clearly "rescale so the dominant component becomes 1", which needs max of *magnitudes*. I worked all four diagonals: UP_RIGHT (0.707,-0.707) → push (-w,+h), correct; DOWN_LEFT → (+w,-h), correct; DOWN_RIGHT → (-w,-h), correct; UP_LEFT (-0.707,-0.707) → `larger = -0.707`, `direction = (1,1)`, push `(-w,-h)` — up and left, i.e. *deeper into* the collidee. Only that one quadrant is wrong. For non-45° directions the divisor can also be the smaller magnitude, over-scaling the displacement.

Separately, after the division `direction` is (±1,±1), so the offset applies the full `inter.width` **and** the full `inter.height` — every diagonal contact is over-resolved on both axes rather than displaced along the axis of least penetration.

CORRECTION to several reviewers: they claimed this wrongly drives Player's branch choice and shoves the player deeper. It does not. The only live path that passes a diagonal is `calculate_object_collision_depth`, which operates on a `clone()` (line 275) and whose caller only reads `amount.abs_x_greater_than_y()` — sign-insensitive. Every direct `resolve_object_collision` call in Player.cpp passes a cardinal direction, where the `x != 0 && y != 0` guard skips the bug. So this is currently a latent defect in a public engine API, not an observable gameplay bug — hence medium, not critical.

```cpp
// calculate the direction of the greatest distance between the two shapes
float x_distance = collider_center.x - collidee_center.x;
float y_distance = collider_center.y - collidee_center.y;
if (std::abs(x_distance) < std::abs(y_distance))
{
	return compare_point_collision_depth_horizontal(collider_center, collidee_center);
}
return compare_point_collision_depth_vertical(collider_center, collidee_center);
```
When |dx| < |dy| the dominant separation is vertical, so by the stated intent the vertical helper belongs here — the branches are inverted. Two shapes whose centres are separated almost entirely in y are reported as a left/right collision. This is the fallback for both the full-containment case (line 154) and the catch-all at line 206 (collider contains collidee, which reaches 206 with all four edge flags false).

The variables are also misnamed: `x_distance`/`y_distance` are signed deltas whose signs are never used.

```cpp
std::unique_ptr<Shape> collider_copy = collider->clone();
Vector2F amount;
resolve_object_collision(collider_copy.get(), collidee, collision_direction, amount);
return amount;
```
`Shape::clone()` is virtual returning `unique_ptr` (MattMath.h:85), so this is an allocate + vtable dispatch + free per query — and for RectangleRotated a second allocation, since it stores its corners in a `std::vector<Point2F>` member. Player calls it on every diagonal contact (Player.cpp:578, 592, 610, 628), inside the quadratic pair loops of Level::update_level_logic (Level.cpp:180-300).

What it costs versus what it produces: the callers use the result only for `amount.abs_x_greater_than_y()` — one boolean — and then call `on_top_collision`/`on_left_collision`, which run `resolve_object_collision` a second time. For two rectangles that boolean is four subtractions from `RectangleF::intersection`, which `resolve_object_AABB_collision` already computes at line 214.

The inner bool return is also discarded, so a genuinely non-colliding pair returns a zero vector indistinguishable from a zero-depth touch.

```cpp
bool MText::is_visible_in_viewport(const RectangleF& view) const
{
	// TODO: implement text bounding box
	return true;
}
```
Byte-identical body at Mh.cpp:343-347 for MTextDropShadow. `view` is unused (C4100 under /W4).

This compounds with the MTexture recursion bug: of the three concrete widgets, one stack-overflows when culled and two silently refuse to be culled, so the is_visible_in_viewport contract inherited from IGameObject is honoured by none of the UI classes. Because MContainer::is_visible_in_viewport (Mh.cpp:113-123) short-circuits on the first child returning true, a single MText anywhere in a container makes the entire container unconditionally visible — the failure mode is "culling appears to work but does no work".

```cpp
Vector2F scale_factor = new_resolution / prev_resolution;
this->scale_size_and_position(scale_factor);
```
Component-wise division with no check that prev_resolution is non-zero. A zero component yields inf or NaN, which scale_size_and_position then multiplies into every child's rectangle, position, scale and shadow offset (Mh.cpp:180-183, 252-256, 315-321). Once a widget's geometry is NaN nothing resets it, so the menu is permanently invisible and the corruption survives the next legitimate resize.

Severity lowered from the reviewers' "medium": I did not establish that a zero resolution is actually reachable (that depends on ResolutionManager and the window-resize path, which I did not audit). The missing guard on a public method is real regardless.

A repo-wide grep confirms remove_child(const std::string&), remove_child(const MObject*), remove_all_children(), get_child_count() and get_children() have **zero** callers outside Mh.cpp — only scale_objects_to_new_resolution is used externally (26 sites). Containers are populated once in init() and never modified.

That matters beyond dead code: these are precisely the methods whose semantics the raw-pointer ownership model leaves undefined. remove_child erases the pointer without deleting, so whether it leaks depends on a convention nobody wrote down.

```cpp
std::vector<std::pair<std::string, MObject*>> MContainer::get_children()
{
	return this->_children;
}
```
returns the whole vector by value (a std::string copy per child) and is not const, so a `const MContainer&` cannot enumerate its children at all — while the neighbouring get_child_count() *is* const.

Both remove_child overloads are hand-written iterator loops differing only in the compared field (Mh.cpp:44-65), where two std::find_if calls would do. The file uses no <algorithm> at all.

Merges 5 candidates. `update_and_get_player_inputs` only pushes an entry for pads where `current[i].connected` is true (:129-134), discarding the pad index. The sole gameplay consumer indexes it by the player's slot with unchecked `operator[]`:

```
int player_index = 0;
for (const auto& object : *this->_player_objects) {
    object->set_player_input(player_inputs[player_index]);   // Level.cpp:192
    ... player_index++;
}
```

Two failures. (1) Identity shift: if pad 0 drops, pad 1 drives player 0. (2) Out-of-range read: `player_inputs.size() < _player_objects->size()` after a drop-out, so `player_inputs[player_index]` reads past the end — UB, garbage `x_movement`/`jump_pressed`/`toggle_pause_menu` copied into `Player::_input`.

The escape hatch is dead: `PlayerInputData::connection` defaults to `DISCONNECTED` (PlayerInputData.h:19) but `calculate_player_input` unconditionally sets `CONNECTED` (PlayerInput.cpp:111), and disconnected pads are never emitted, so no consumer can ever observe a drop-out. `GameLevel::check_for_pause_input` (GameStates.cpp:348-359) returns the vector index and it is used as a player number for the pause-menu owner, so that is wrong too after a drop-out (it does at least bound its own loop by `.size()`).

Secondary, also verified: on reconnect `_prev_inputs[i]` was overwritten with a default-constructed `RawPlayerInput` at :135 during the disconnected frames, so a button held across the disconnect reads as a fresh press.

The constructor ends with `this->_sound_bank = resource_manager->get_sound_bank(SOUND_BANK);` (:44) into the raw non-owning `SoundBank* _sound_bank = nullptr;` (Player.h:168). The owner is `ResourceManager::_sound_banks`, a map of `unique_ptr<SoundBank>`.

`Game::OnDeviceLost()` (Game.cpp:269-280) calls `_resource_manager->reset_all_sounds()`, which is:
```
for (auto& sound_bank : this->_sound_banks) {
    sound_bank.second->reset_all_instances();
    sound_bank.second.reset();          // ResourceManager.cpp:124 — destroys the SoundBank
}
```
OnDeviceLost does not tear down the state machine (only `_sprite_batches`, `_states`, textures, fonts, sounds), so every live `Player` keeps a dangling `SoundBank*`. The next `_sound_bank->play_wave(...)` — Player.cpp:687 (damage), 707 (death), 880 (jump), 1002 (respawn) — is a use-after-free. `OnDeviceRestored` re-runs `load_all_resources()` and inserts NEW SoundBank objects, so nothing re-fetches the pointer and the dangle is permanent, not transient.

Graded high rather than critical only because it requires a device-lost event; the consequence when it happens is memory corruption.

Merges 2 candidates. `on_structure_ramp_collision` dispatches on ramp type and the left-ramp arm is literally empty:
```
if (other_type == collision_object_type::STRUCTURE_RAMP_LEFT)
{
    // TODO
}
else if (other_type == collision_object_type::STRUCTURE_RAMP_RIGHT) { ...four near-identical cases... }
```
Anything not caught by `bottom_edge_collision` (UP/UP_LEFT/UP_RIGHT) or `side_wall_collision` (RIGHT && RAMP_LEFT) at :254-269 falls into the empty branch: no `resolve_object_collision`, no `set_move_state`, no velocity zeroing. The player passes through. Worse, `Level::update_level_logic` counts it as a structure contact anyway (Level.cpp:238-241 checks only `is_structure(...)`), so `on_no_collision()` is suppressed and the move state freezes.

Latent today — the only shipped ramp is `STRUCTURE_RAMP_RIGHT` in king_of_the_hill.json — but `LevelObjectBuilder.cpp:152-155` happily accepts the string `"STRUCTURE_RAMP_LEFT"` from JSON, so the first left ramp anyone authors becomes an invisible hole with no assert, log or throw. The half-built `StructureRampLeft.h/.cpp` are entirely commented out yet still listed in the vcxproj, so there is no compile-time signal either.

The RAMP_RIGHT arm is also four copy-pasted identical three-statement bodies (:280-345) differing only in the direction being matched.

```
void Player::on_no_collision() {
    player_move_state move_state = this->get_move_state();
    if (move_state == ON_GROUND || move_state == ON_DROP_DOWN_GROUND ||
        move_state == ON_RAMP_LEFT || move_state == ON_RAMP_RIGHT)
    { this->set_move_state(player_move_state::IN_AIR); }
}
```
`ON_CEILING` is set by `on_top_collision` (:551) and is as much a surface-contact state as the four listed, but is missing. Once a player bumps their head, `_move_state` stays `ON_CEILING` until they land and `on_bottom_collision` overwrites it. Observable while falling: `calculate_animation_state` returns IDLE for ON_CEILING (:1023-1026) so the idle clip plays while plummeting; `do_jump` has an explicit `else if (move_state == ON_CEILING) { // do nothing }` arm (:882-885) so no jump bookkeeping runs at all; and the debug overlay reports `on_ceiling` throughout.

Related, root cause in Level but the semantics are Player's: `player_colliding_with_structure` (Level.cpp:224-248) is set for ANY structure contact including a pure side-wall hit, and `on_left_collision`/`on_right_collision` (:562-575) never touch the move state. So a player who walks off a ledge while brushing a wall never gets `on_no_collision()` and keeps `ON_GROUND` in mid-air, which does grant a free jump.

Merges 2 candidates.
```
// pause menu is on key press
bool pause_menu = !current.pause_menu && previous.pause_menu;
// show debug is on key press
bool show_debug = !current.show_debug && previous.show_debug;
```
`!current && previous` is the FALLING edge — the button was down last frame and is up now. That is release, not press. The correct rising-edge test is what the jump handling three lines above gets right: `bool jump_button_pressed = current.jump_button && !previous.jump_button;` (:45). So Start and Back are inverted relative to A: the pause menu opens when you let go of Start, and the debug toggle (consumed at Player.cpp:726-729) fires on release of Back.

Graded low rather than medium because it is functional — a one-frame-plus-hold-duration lag, arguably even deliberate for the pause menu to avoid double-triggering into the menu. But the comments explicitly claim 'key press', and being inconsistent with the jump edge detection in the same function is a maintenance trap.

The surrounding shape is also noise: three consecutive `if (x) { result.y = true; } else { result.y = false; }` blocks (:54-84) — 27 lines that say `result.y = x;` three times, in a function whose other assignments (:43-48) are already written directly. Line 82's `else` brace is indented to column 0.

Merges 2 candidates.
```
const std::vector<Vector2F> team_a_spawns = load_info->get_team_a_spawns();
const std::vector<Vector2F> team_b_spawns = load_info->get_team_b_spawns();
...
for (auto& setting : settings.get_player_settings())
```
`LevelLoadedInfo::get_team_a_spawns()`/`get_team_b_spawns()` (LevelLoadedInfo.h:17-18) and `MenuLevelSettings::get_player_settings()` (MenuLevelSettings.h:14) all return their members by value, so each call deep-copies a vector.

The range-for then binds `auto&` (non-const) into a lifetime-extended temporary. That is legal — the range-for's `auto&& __range` extends it — but it is exactly the shape that becomes a dangling-reference bug when someone hoists the call or extracts the loop body into a helper, and the non-const `auto&` falsely suggests the settings are being modified in place (any mutation would be silently discarded).

```
void Player::draw(SpriteBatch* sprite_batch) {
    AnimationObject::draw(sprite_batch, this->_rectangle, Camera::DEFAULT_CAMERA);
    this->_primary->draw(sprite_batch, Camera::DEFAULT_CAMERA);
}
```
Compared with the camera overload (:47-90) it is missing three things: the `TextureObject` uniform pass (so the player renders with no team colour — the only visual that distinguishes team A from team B), the `SpriteEffects_FlipHorizontally` derived from `get_facing_right()` (so the player always faces right), and the `_state == DEAD` early-return (so a dead player is drawn).

A straightforward copy-paste divergence between two overrides of the same virtual pair. Latent — `Level` only ever calls the camera overload — but any engine code drawing an `IGameObject*` without a camera gets a silently wrong player.

```
this->_proj_builder = std::make_unique<ProjectileBuilder>();
this->_sound_bank = resource_manager->get_sound_bank(details.sound_bank_name);
```
Neither `resource_manager` nor the result is checked. `ResourceManager::get_sound_bank` (ResourceManager.cpp:90-101) is `try { return this->_sound_banks[sound_bank_name].get(); } catch (const std::out_of_range&) { ... }`. `std::map::operator[]` **default-inserts a null unique_ptr and never throws out_of_range**, so the catch is dead code, a bad name silently yields `nullptr` and pollutes the map. `_sound_bank` is then dereferenced with no guard at Weapon.cpp:242, :249, :256, WeaponSniper.cpp:32 and WeaponBucket.cpp:32 - a null deref on the first frame the trigger is pressed. `weapon_consts.h` hand-writes `"sound_bank_1"` in six places (:25, :47, :81, :97, :125, :159); one typo becomes a crash with nothing pointing at the cause. The identical `operator[]` pattern is in `get_sprite_sheet` (ResourceManager.cpp:46-58).

```
if (add_player_vel == add_player_velocity::X_AND_Y) { result += player_velocity * player_vel_amount; }
else if (add_player_vel == add_player_velocity::X_ONLY) { result.x += player_velocity.x * player_vel_amount; }
else //if (add_player_vel == add_player_velocity::Y_ONLY)
{ result.y += player_velocity.y * player_vel_amount; }
```
`add_player_velocity` has four enumerators (WeaponDetails.h:7-13) - the fourth is `NONE`. A weapon configured with NONE, meaning "do not inherit owner velocity", instead gets the owner's Y velocity added. The commented-out `//if (... == Y_ONLY)` shows the author believed the chain exhaustive. `weapon_consts::DETAILS_RELATIVE_DEFAULT` (weapon_consts.h:30-34) is exactly `{ NONE, 1.0f }` and is the NSDMI of `RelativeVelocityWeapon::_rel_details` (Weapon.h:182). Latent today only because all three live relative weapons pass `X_ONLY` (weapon_consts.h:54, :132, :166) and the NSDMI is always overwritten by the constructor - but the failure would be silent, projectiles just flying at the wrong angle.

```
struct SoundEffectInstanceWeaponDetails {
    std::string shoot_sound_name_a0 = ""; ... shoot_sound_name_b3 = "";
    const std::string& get_sound_name(player_team team, int index) const;
};
```
`WeaponDetails.cpp:4-40` resolves it with a `switch(team)` wrapping two `switch(index)` blocks - 36 lines, eight returns, three `throw std::exception`. The maximum player count is baked into *struct field names*, and the same 2x4 shape is repeated three times in weapon_consts.h (:58-68, :102-112, :136-146) where every entry is mechanically `"<weapon>_<team><index>"`. Supporting a fifth player or a third team means editing a struct, a 36-line accessor, and three positional aggregate tables in which one misplaced line silently gives team B's sound to team A. `int index` is an unchecked raw index in a public API with no documented range, and the function is called on *every* update of a sprayer/roller/mister (Weapon.cpp:238), so any bad index is an uncaught throw out of the render loop rather than a missing sound.

```
protected:
	SoundBank* _sound_bank = nullptr;
	WeaponDetails _details = weapon_consts::DETAILS_DEFAULT;
	bool _shooting_this_update = false;
```
These are protected *data*, not accessors, and subclasses reach straight into them: WeaponSniper.cpp:32-33 and WeaponBucket.cpp:32-33 both do `this->_sound_bank->play_wave(this->_details.shoot_sound_name, this->_details.shoot_sound_volume);`, and WeaponRoller.cpp:90 reads `this->_shooting_this_update` - despite `get_details()` (Weapon.h:64) already existing and returning `const WeaponDetails&`. So the class offers a controlled accessor and an uncontrolled back door for the same field, and a subclass can repoint `_sound_bank` or rewrite `_details` after construction with the base unable to notice. `_details` is also a per-instance by-value copy (Weapon.cpp:23) of an immutable shared table entry - five `std::string`s duplicated into every weapon of every player, never mutated after construction (every accessor at Weapon.cpp:393-421 is a const read).

findings.suggestion:
Compare like with like and only zero the velocity when the drag step would overshoot through zero:
```cpp
const float vx = get_velocity_x();
const float delta = wind_resistance * dt;
if (std::fabs(vx) <= delta) set_velocity_x(0.0f);
else                        alter_velocity_x(vx > 0.0f ? -delta : delta);
```
That removes the magic `4.0f` and the discontinuity. If exponential drag is wanted, `vx *= std::pow(damping, dt)`. Move `MAX_VELOCITY` into `ProjectileDetails` so each type caps itself, and factor the four ladders into one helper.

findings.detail:
Verified. The factory builds the rect at the raw nozzle position — `RectangleF(position, DETAILS_SPRAY.col_rect_size)` (ProjectileBuilder.cpp:30, and equivalents at :48, :66, :98, :134-190) — and then every constructor separately re-reads the same constant out of `get_details()` and shifts it to centre:
```cpp
Vector2F size = this->get_details().col_rect_size;
this->_rectangle.offset(-size.x / 2.0f, -size.y / 2.0f);
```
verbatim in ProjectileSpray.cpp:25-27, ProjectileJet.cpp:25-27, ProjectileBall.cpp:25-27, ProjectileMist.cpp:25-27 and ProjectileRolling.cpp:25-27. One conceptual operation — 'place a rect of size S centred on P' — spread across two files and six copies, with the caller required to know the callee will re-centre. The local also copies the `Vector2F` instead of binding a const reference, and the constructor calls a base accessor during its own construction to recover data it was itself handed.

Verified:
```cpp
void ProjectileRolling::draw(SpriteBatch* sprite_batch, const Camera& camera) { /* do nothing */ }
void ProjectileRolling::draw(SpriteBatch* sprite_batch)                      { /* do nothing */ }
bool ProjectileRolling::is_visible_in_viewport(const RectangleF& view) const  { return false; }
```
Four unreferenced parameters. The project enables `WarningLevel Level4` in all four configurations (ArtAttack.vcxproj:91, 113, 134, 160) but does not treat warnings as errors, so C4100 accumulates silently — the unused `type` parameter at ProjectileBuilder.cpp:123 is the same warning.

Separately, this class still inherits `AnimationObject`, and its constructor resolves `DETAILS_ROLLING.sheet_name`/`animation_strip_name` ("sprite_sheet_1"/"projectile_3", projectile_consts.h:70-71) through `ResourceManager` at AnimationObject.cpp:21 — a full sprite-sheet lookup and animation state for an object that is never drawn.

```cpp
struct PaintTotal { float team_a = 0.0f; float team_b = 0.0f; };
class IPaintableGameObject { virtual PaintTotal get_paint_total() const = 0; };
```
A core object interface that hardcodes (a) that scoring is "paint", (b) that there are exactly two teams, (c) that they are named A and B. The assumption propagates into `LevelEndInfo` (LevelEndInfo.h:10-11), `Level::get_level_end_info` (Level.cpp:706-735), and the `*_TEAM_A`/`*_TEAM_B` cross-product in `collision_object_type` (collision_object_type.h:11-24, 23 enumerators encoding class × team × weapon).

Adding a third team, or any non-area scoring rule, means editing the engine's core collision enum, the paint interface, the scoring struct and the end-info formatter. For a project whose stated goal is a reusable engine, this interface should not exist in engine code at all.

`draw_active_level` (Level.cpp:379-399) partitions **players** across thread-pool tasks, but each task's `draw_player_view_level` iterates over **all** shared lists: `_non_collision_objects` (:516), `_collision_objects` (:525), `_player_objects` (:534), calling `object->draw(...)`.

`IGameObject::draw` is non-const (IGameObject.h:11-12) and `PaintTile::draw` genuinely mutates the object (PaintTile.cpp:34-53):
```cpp
this->set_colour(this->_team_colours.get_team_colour(player_team::A));
this->_splash.set_colour(...);
this->_splash.set_rectangle_center(this->_rectangle.get_center());
```
`set_colour` writes `DrawObject::_colour`; `set_rectangle_center` writes `PaintTileSplash::_rectangle` (PaintTile.cpp:172-175). With 2+ players there are 2+ worker threads executing `StructurePaintable::draw` → `PaintTile::draw` on the *same* tile objects. Non-atomic concurrent writes, no synchronisation anywhere on the path.

Secondary race on the same path: `SpriteSheet::draw` uses `this->_sprite_frames[frame_name]` (SpriteSheet.cpp:105 and :126) — `std::map::operator[]`, which **inserts** on a miss and can restructure the tree while other threads read it.

Note this is entirely avoidable: `TextureObject`'s draw primitives are already `const` (TextureObject.h:20-32). `PaintTile::draw` is the reason the interface is non-const, and the colour it recomputes every frame is fully determined by `_team`, which only changes in `on_collision` (PaintTile.cpp:138). Candidates 59 and 77 merged.

Same function as the layout finding, different defect. `draw_zoom_out_level_component` runs inside a thread-pool task (queued at Level.cpp:601-604) and calls the **single-argument** `apply_player_viewport(0)`. That overload (ViewportManager.cpp:63-70) does:
```cpp
auto context = this->_device_resources->GetD3DDeviceContext();
context->RSSetViewports(1, &vp);
this->_sprite_batch->SetViewport(vp);
```
i.e. it touches the **immediate** context and ViewportManager's own shared `SpriteBatch` — while the main thread owns that immediate context inside `Game::render()`. `ID3D11DeviceContext` is explicitly not free-threaded.

Worse, neither of those objects is the one being recorded into: every draw in the function goes to `sprite_batches->at(0)` on `deferred_contexts->at(0)` (Level.cpp:624-646). So the viewport the function intends to establish never reaches the command list it produces. It renders correctly today only because `Game::clear` sets the fullscreen viewport on every deferred context each frame (Game.cpp:136-148) and the overview happens to want fullscreen.

Contrast the per-player path, Level.cpp:505-506, which correctly uses `apply_player_viewport(player_num, deferred_contexts->at(i), sprite_batches->at(i))`.

`update_level_logic` walks `_player_objects` and indexes the input vector by loop position:
```cpp
int player_index = 0;
for (const auto& object : *this->_player_objects)
{
    object->set_player_input(player_inputs[player_index]);
```
`PlayerInput::update_and_get_player_inputs()` (PlayerInput.cpp:119-138) **compacts** — it only `push_back`s for pads where `current[i].connected`:
```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++)
    if (current[i].connected) result.push_back(calculate_player_input(current[i], _prev_inputs[i]));
```
Two bugs follow. (1) If any pad disconnects mid-match, `player_inputs.size() < _player_objects->size()` and `player_inputs[player_index]` reads past the end — `operator[]` is unchecked, so silent UB. (2) Even without a size mismatch, if pad 1 of 0..3 drops while 0, 2, 3 stay connected, entries shift down and player object 1 is driven by pad 2.

The same loop also uses `player_index` for `_viewport_manager->get_player_viewport(player_index)` (Level.cpp:198), while `draw_player_view_level` uses `object->get_player_num()` for the same lookup (Level.cpp:503-511). Two indexing schemes that agree only by coincidence.

```cpp
bool player_colliding_with_structure = false;
for (auto& other_object : *this->_collision_objects) {
    if (other_object->get_for_deletion()) continue;
    if (player->is_colliding(other_object.get())) {
        player->on_collision(other_object.get());
        other_object->on_collision(player.get());
        if (is_structure(other_object->get_collision_object_type()))
            player_colliding_with_structure = true;
    }
}
if (!player_colliding_with_structure) player->on_no_collision();
```
`Player::on_no_collision` (Player.cpp:1252-1263) is the *only* thing that moves the player out of ON_GROUND/ON_DROP_DOWN_GROUND/ON_RAMP_* into IN_AIR. But the flag is set for any structure that merely overlaps, regardless of how the collision resolved:
- `Player::on_left_collision` / `on_right_collision` (Player.cpp:562-575) zero velocity_x and push out but deliberately never touch move_state. A player who walks off a ledge while still brushing a wall keeps move_state == ON_GROUND and can jump in mid-air while hugging the wall.
- `Player::on_structure_jump_through_collision` (Player.cpp:360-382) returns without resolving when the player is rising through or dropping through the platform — yet `is_structure(STRUCTURE_JUMP_THROUGH)` is true, so the flag is set and the player is treated as grounded while passing *through* a one-way platform.

The flag is also computed over the pre-resolution overlap set, so an object that only overlapped before push-out still counts. Ground state is game logic; it should be produced by the collision responses, not inferred by the loop driver.

```cpp
for (auto& player : *this->_player_objects)
    if (this->is_object_out_of_bounds(player.get()))
        throw std::exception("Player out of bounds");
for (auto& object : *this->_collision_objects)
    if (this->is_object_out_of_bounds(object.get()))
        throw std::exception("Collision object out of bounds");
```
Nothing catches this. I grepped Main.cpp: it has no `try` or `catch` anywhere, and neither `Game::tick` nor `GameLevel::update` wraps the call. So a projectile clearing the out-of-bounds rectangle terminates the process mid-match. `is_object_out_of_bounds` (Level.cpp:689-694) fires the moment an object's AABB stops intersecting the level's out-of-bounds rect — an entirely ordinary event for a fast projectile in a shooter.

The engine already has a despawn mechanism (`get_for_deletion` + the swap-erase at Level.cpp:296-305) that this check declines to use. It is also a redundant second O(n) traversal of `_collision_objects` immediately after that sweep.

Note the two stored-but-never-read spawn vectors (`_team_a_spawns`/`_team_b_spawns`, Level.h:136-137) are exactly what the player case should use to respawn instead of throwing.

```cpp
void StructurePaintable::draw(SpriteBatch* sprite_batch, const Camera& camera)
{
    Structure::draw(sprite_batch, camera);
    for (auto& paint_tile : this->_paint_tiles)
        paint_tile.draw(sprite_batch, camera);   // no visibility test
}
```
`Level::draw_player_view_level` culls at the *structure* level (`object->is_visible_in_viewport(camera_view)`, Level.cpp:527), which for a StructurePaintable tests only the parent sprite rectangle (Structure.cpp:39-42). Once one pixel of the parent is on screen, all tiles are submitted. `PaintTile::is_visible_in_viewport` exists (PaintTile.cpp:149-152) and is never called by anything.

The scale is real: I parsed the shipped levels. `boundary_floor` in both king_of_the_hill.json and turbulence.json is a single 6000x1000 StructurePaintable with `top: true`, which at WIDTH=4.0f generates **1,500 tiles** on one object that spans the whole map and is therefore always "visible". Every painted tile issues two sprites (splash + tile), and each sprite does a string-keyed `std::map` lookup in `SpriteSheet::draw` (SpriteSheet.cpp:105/126) plus another for the sheet itself. Multiply by up to 4 viewports per frame.

```cpp
bool tile_painted = false;
for (auto& paint_tile : this->_paint_tiles)
{
    if (paint_tile.is_colliding(other))
    {
        paint_tile.on_collision(other);
        if (!tile_painted) this->_sound_bank->play_wave(SOUND_NAME, SOUND_VOLUME);
        tile_painted = true;
    }
}
```
The tiles sit on a strictly regular grid (generated at StructurePaintable.cpp:221-296 as `get_left() + paint_tile_width * i`), so the small set a projectile can overlap is computable in O(1) from its AABB — yet this scans all of them. For `boundary_floor` (1,500 tiles, confirmed from the shipped JSON) that is 1,500 `PaintTile::is_colliding` calls per projectile hit, each doing a virtual `get_shape()`, two by-value `get_bounding_box()` calls, and possibly a `Shape::intersects` dynamic_cast chain.

The `tile_painted` flag only dedupes *within one* `on_collision` call, and `on_collision` fires once per colliding projectile, so N projectiles hitting the floor in one frame issue N one-shot voices.

```cpp
const int num_paint_tiles_x = static_cast<int>(std::fabs(this->get_rectangle().get_width() / WIDTH));
const float paint_tile_width = this->get_rectangle().get_width() / num_paint_tiles_x;
const int num_paint_tiles_y = static_cast<int>(std::fabs(this->get_rectangle().get_height() / HEIGHT));
const float paint_tile_height = this->get_rectangle().get_height() / num_paint_tiles_y;
```
`WIDTH`/`HEIGHT` are 4.0f (PaintTile.h:12-13). Any paintable structure narrower or shorter than 4 units truncates the count to 0 and the next line divides by it. `FloatingPointModel` is `Fast` in all four configurations (ArtAttack.vcxproj:94, 116, 139, 165) and no FP exceptions are enabled, so this silently yields ±inf or NaN rather than trapping. The guarded loops then don't run, so the observable failure is that the structure silently gets no paint surface at all with no diagnostic.

Secondly, `std::fabs` is applied to the *count* but not the *tile size*, so a rectangle with negative width (which `RectangleF::from_top_left_bottom_right`, used at LevelObjectBuilder.cpp:51/94, will happily produce from mis-ordered `rectangle_tlbr` values) yields a positive count and a negative tile width — tiles with negative `get_area()` that subtract from a team's score.

Nothing between the JSON and this code validates width/height (LevelObjectBuilder.cpp:80-99 accepts any float). Latent today — every shipped structure is ≥ 25 units — but it is an unguarded divide on externally supplied data.

```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < edges.size(); i++)
    for (int j = 0; j < this->_paintable_edges.size(); j++)
        if (edges[i] == this->_paintable_edges[j])
```
`Segment::operator==` (MattMath.cpp:4045-4049) forwards to `Vector2F::operator==` (MattMath.cpp:1574-1577), which is bare `this->x == other.x && this->y == other.y` on floats.

This works today only because LevelObjectBuilder.cpp:107-125 derives `paintable_edges` from the *same* `RectangleF` value that is also passed as the collision shape (LevelObjectBuilder.cpp:135-136), so the bit patterns are identical. The moment the collision shape differs from the sprite rectangle by one ULP — a rotated shape, a shrunk collision box, anything built via subtraction — *zero* edges match and the structure silently generates no paint tiles. There is no diagnostic for the all-unmatched case, and the loops don't break out once a match is found.

The loops are also `int i < edges.size()`, a signed/unsigned comparison (C4018 under /W4).

This is the same root cause as the face-index bug: geometry is being used to smuggle four booleans.

```cpp
if (type == collision_object_type::STRUCTURE_PAINTABLE)
{
    try {
        auto paintable_object = dynamic_cast<IPaintableGameObject*>(object.get());
        PaintTotal paint = paintable_object->get_paint_total();   // :722, unguarded
        ...
    }
    catch (const std::bad_cast& e) { throw std::exception("Could not cast to IPaintableGameObject"); }
}
```
The **pointer** form of `dynamic_cast` returns `nullptr` on failure; only the reference form throws `std::bad_cast`. The catch block is unreachable, `e` is an unused variable (C4101 under /W4), and the failure it was written to handle — an object tagged STRUCTURE_PAINTABLE that isn't a StructurePaintable — is an unchecked null dereference at line 722.

The guard is only the enum tag from `Structure::get_collision_object_type()` (Structure.cpp:51-54), which is just whatever `collision_type` the constructor was handed. LevelObjectBuilder.cpp:71-78 happens to pair them correctly today, so this is not currently triggerable — hence medium, not high — but the enum is being used as a substitute for the type system with a null-unsafe cast to recover the type. This is also the whole scoring path: a failure here loses the match result. Candidates 4, 22, 40, 61 and 84 merged.

```cpp
float winning_ratio()  const { return winning_score() / losing_score(); }
float losing_ratio()   const { return losing_score() / winning_score(); }
float winning_percentage() const { return 100.0f * winning_ratio(); }
float losing_percentage()  const { return 100.0f - winning_percentage(); }
```
Three defects. (1) The ratio is winner/loser, not winner/total, so a 70/30 match reports `winning_percentage()` = 233% and `losing_percentage()` = -133%. The sibling `team_a_percentage()` (line 34-37) uses the correct share-of-total definition, so two contradictory meanings of "percentage" live in one struct. (2) Division by zero whenever the loser painted nothing — and in the nobody-painted case `winning_team()` returns NONE, both `winning_score()` and `losing_score()` return `team_a_area` = 0, giving 0/0 = NaN under `/fp:fast`. (3) `losing_score()` on a draw returns `team_a_area` (line 107-110), the same value `winning_score()` returns, which is not "the losing score" under any reading.

I grepped the whole tree: `winning_ratio`, `losing_ratio`, `winning_percentage` and `losing_percentage` have no callers outside each other. So this is a loaded gun, not a live crash — hence medium.

Same file: the `throw std::exception("No winning team")` at lines 77, 94 and 111 is unreachable (the preceding chain covers all three `player_team` values) and exists only to silence "not all control paths return a value". `team_a_ratio`/`team_b_ratio` (lines 18-33) also guard with exact float `== 0.0f` on each field rather than on the sum.

`_countdown_text` (Level.h:111) is null after construction. It is allocated only inside the `_start_timer >= START_TIMER` branch of the first `update()` (Level.cpp:95-104) — a condition true exactly once — and dereferenced with no guard at :109, :114 and, crucially, from the draw path:
```cpp
// draw_player_view_level, Level.cpp:581-585
if (_state == level_state::START_COUNTDOWN || _start_timer > -1.0f)
    this->draw_countdown_text(sprite_batches->at(i), viewport_camera);
// draw_countdown_text, Level.cpp:742
this->_countdown_text->draw(sprite_batch, viewport_camera);
```
The only thing preventing a null deref on the first rendered frame is `game_level_state::FIRST_UPDATE`/`SECOND_UPDATE` (GameStates.h:38-39) — two enum values whose entire bodies are "advance to the next state" (GameStates.cpp:133-138) paired with an early return in draw (GameStates.cpp:305-309). That is an undocumented ordering contract expressed as dummy FSM states, and it must be re-established by hand on every restart (GameStates.cpp:204, :281).

The public `Level::set_state(level_state)` (Level.h:88, zero callers) additionally lets any client put the object back into START_COUNTDOWN, re-entering the branch at :67 without re-satisfying the `_start_timer` condition.

I checked the specific crash scenario one reviewer proposed (pause pressed on the SECOND_UPDATE frame) — see the rejected finding; it is not reachable as described. Downgraded from high to medium accordingly: this is a latent precondition, not a demonstrated crash.

```cpp
auto partitioned_players = this->_partitioner->partition(this->_player_objects->size(), num_threads);
for (int i = 0; i < partitioned_players.size(); i++)
{
    this->_thread_pool->add_task([this, i, partitioned_players,   // captured BY VALUE
        deferred_contexts, command_lists, sprite_batches]() {
            this->draw_player_view_level(partitioned_players[i].first, partitioned_players[i].second, ...);
        });
}
```
`partitioned_players` is a `std::vector<std::pair<int,int>>` (Partitioner.h:11) captured by value, so a full vector copy is heap-allocated per task per frame — and each lambda only reads element `i`. `ThreadPool::add_task` then heap-allocates the closure again (`new std::function<void()>(task)`, ThreadPool.cpp:41). `Partitioner::partition` (Partitioner.cpp:4-22) also `push_back`s with no `reserve`. That is roughly 1 + 2N allocations per frame on the render path for data identical every frame while the player count is unchanged.

The callee side compounds it: `GameLevel::draw` allocates a 16-element `std::vector<ID3D11CommandList*>` every frame (GameStates.cpp:313) because of Level::draw's out-parameter signature.

Also `int i < partitioned_players.size()` is a signed/unsigned comparison — C4018 under /W4, which is enabled (ArtAttack.vcxproj:91 etc.) but not treated as an error anywhere. Same at StructurePaintable.cpp:189/191 and GameStates.cpp:321.

All four object lists are double-owned:
```cpp
std::unique_ptr<std::vector<std::unique_ptr<IGameObject>>>            _non_collision_objects = nullptr;
std::unique_ptr<std::vector<std::unique_ptr<ICollisionGameObject>>>   _collision_objects = nullptr;
std::unique_ptr<std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Player>>>                 _player_objects = nullptr;
std::unique_ptr<std::vector<std::unique_ptr<IGameObject>>>            _viewport_dividers = nullptr;
```
The same types are the constructor parameters (Level.h:58-61) and the return types of `LevelObjectBuilder::build_collision_objects`/`build_non_collision_objects`/`build_viewport_dividers` (LevelObjectBuilder.h:19-27).

`std::vector` already is the sole, cheaply-movable owner of its buffer. The outer `unique_ptr` buys an extra heap allocation and indirection on every access, plus a nullable state that ~20 dereference sites in Level.cpp assume away (:183, 190, 216, 222, 259, 277, 296, 308, 315, 516, 525, 534, 548, 629, 634, 639, 661, 711).

Most importantly it is what makes the `const` on `update_level_logic(...) const` (Level.cpp:180) meaningless: the method mutates every object, moves players, and appends spawned projectiles at Level.cpp:209 (`this->_collision_objects->push_back(std::move(proj))`) — the outer pointer being const says nothing about the vector. `draw_active_level(...) const` (Level.cpp:375) likewise queues thread-pool work. `const` here conveys nothing to a reader and nothing to the compiler. Candidates 30, 53 and 68 merged.

```cpp
SoundBank* ResourceManager::get_sound_bank(const std::string& sound_bank_name)
{
    try { return this->_sound_banks[sound_bank_name].get(); }
    catch (const std::out_of_range&) { ... throw std::out_of_range(error); }
}
```
`std::map::operator[]` never throws `out_of_range` — it **default-inserts** a null `unique_ptr<SoundBank>` for the missing key and returns `nullptr`. The catch is unreachable dead code and the map is silently polluted with a null entry. Contrast the correct sibling `get_level_info` (ResourceManager.cpp:66-78), which uses `.at()` inside the same try/catch shape.

The name comes straight from level JSON: `LevelLoadedInfo::get_sound_bank_name()` (LevelLoadedInfo.cpp:72-75) → LevelBuilder.cpp:72 → `Level::Level` stores the result at Level.cpp:59 with no check. The first `update()` then dereferences it at Level.cpp:73 (`_sound_bank->play_effect(...)`) — a null deref with no diagnostic. `StructurePaintable.cpp:34` has the same exposure with the hardcoded `"sound_bank_1"`.

I am reporting this here rather than under Level because the fix belongs in the accessor, but the level module is the sole consumer and the one that crashes.

```cpp
class PaintTile final : public TextureObject, public ICollisionGameObject
{
    MattMath::RectangleF _rectangle;
    player_team _team = player_team::NONE;
    TeamColour _team_colours = TeamColour();   // identical for every tile in the level
    const float* _dt = nullptr;                // stored and never read
    PaintTileSplash _splash;                   // a whole AnimationObject
};
```
Each tile carries two vtable pointers, a `ResourceManager*`, colour/origin/rotation/effects/depth from `DrawObject`, **two `std::string`s** from `SpriteSheetObject` (`"sprite_sheet_1"`, `"pixel"`), and an embedded `PaintTileSplash` with two more strings (`"sprite_sheet_1"`, `"splash_1"`) plus its own full copy of the DrawObject state. Every one of those strings holds the same literal, per tile. `TeamColour` is a level-wide constant duplicated per tile.

Counts from the shipped data: king_of_the_hill's `boundary_floor` alone is 1,500 tiles; turbulence has that plus eight more paintable structures. `StructurePaintable::update` (:37-43) walks all of them every frame just to tick `_splash.update()`, which for a paused splash is a virtual call that returns immediately.

`_dt` (PaintTile.h:82) is stored from the ctor and never read — I grepped PaintTile.cpp and the only hit is the initialiser at line 23; the splash gets its own copy of the pointer.

`_splash` is also two-phase constructed: default-constructed then assigned in the constructor body (PaintTile.cpp:25-28), which is the only reason `PaintTileSplash() = default` has to exist.

```cpp
void Level::draw_zoom_out_level(...) const
{
    int num_threads = this->_thread_pool->get_max_num_threads();   // :599 — never used
    this->_thread_pool->add_task([this, deferred_contexts, command_lists, sprite_batches]()
        { this->draw_zoom_out_level_component(deferred_contexts, command_lists, sprite_batches); });
    this->_thread_pool->wait_for_tasks_to_complete();
}
```
One task, then an immediate join. Net effect is a `new std::function` allocation (ThreadPool.cpp:41), a CreateThreadpoolWork/SubmitThreadpoolWork/WaitForThreadpoolWorkCallbacks/CloseThreadpoolWork round trip and a thread hand-off, all to run work the calling thread then waits for anyway — strictly worse than calling the function. It also moves the work onto a thread where it must not be (see the immediate-context finding) and onto the wrong side of the terminate-on-throw boundary.

`num_threads` is an unreferenced local — C4189 under /W4, which is enabled but with `TreatWarningAsError` set nowhere in ArtAttack.vcxproj, so it accumulated unseen.

```cpp
for (size_t i = 0; i < this->_collision_objects->size(); i++)
{
    if (this->_collision_objects->at(i)->get_for_deletion())
    {
        this->_collision_objects->at(i) = std::move(this->_collision_objects->back());
        this->_collision_objects->pop_back();
        i--;
    }
}
```
Two things are load-bearing and neither is stated: when `i == size()-1`, `at(i)` and `back()` are the same `unique_ptr`, so line 300 is a **self-move-assignment** (survivable on every mainstream implementation, but it reads like a use-after-move); and when `i == 0` the `i--` wraps a `size_t` to `SIZE_MAX` before `i++` returns it to 0. Defined behaviour, written as if signed. It also uses bounds-checked `at()` inside a loop already guarded by `size()`. No leak — `pop_back()` destroys correctly — so this is fragility, not a bug.

One reviewer claimed no file includes `<algorithm>`; that is **wrong** — pch.h:42 includes it. But I grepped the entire first-party tree for `std::find`, `std::count_if`, `std::remove_if`, `std::accumulate`, `std::any_of`, `std::sort`, `std::for_each` and got **zero** matches, so the underlying point stands: the algorithms are available and simply never used. Other sites that want them: Level.cpp:658-682 (count_if), StructurePaintable.cpp:189-213 (find/any_of — where the face bug lives), StructurePaintable.cpp:303-317 (accumulate; note the stray `;` after the switch's closing brace at :316).

