findings.suggestion:
Use a real buffer and its real size, check the open, RAII the handle:
```cpp
std::vector<char> read_buffer(64 * 1024);
FILE* fp = nullptr;
if (fopen_s(&fp, json_path, "rb") != 0 || !fp)
    throw std::runtime_error(std::string("cannot open ") + json_path);
std::unique_ptr<FILE, decltype(&fclose)> guard(fp, &fclose);
rapidjson::FileReadStream is(fp, read_buffer.data(), read_buffer.size());
rapidjson::Document d;
if (d.ParseStream(is).HasParseError()) throw std::runtime_error(...);
```
Fix SoundBank.cpp:150-161 the same way. For a 14KB file, `Document::Parse` on a slurped string is simpler than streaming at all. Grep the tree for `sizeof(` applied to any smart pointer.

findings.detail:
```cpp
Colour original_color = this->get_colour();
Vector2F original_position = this->get_position();
float original_scale = this->get_scale();
this->TextObject::set_colour(this->_shadow_color);
this->TextObject::set_scale(this->_shadow_scale);
this->TextObject::set_position(this->get_position() + this->_shadow_offset);
this->TextObject::draw(sprite_batch, camera);
this->TextObject::set_colour(original_color);   // restore
this->TextObject::set_scale(original_scale);
this->TextObject::set_position(original_position);
this->TextObject::draw(sprite_batch, camera);
```
Save/mutate/restore at object granularity. Three consequences, all verified:

1. It is the reason `draw` is non-const, which is the direct cause of the override/hiding bug below.
2. It is a live data race. `Level::_countdown_text` is one shared `TextDropShadow`; `Level::draw_countdown_text(...) const` calls `this->_countdown_text->draw(...)` and is invoked from inside `draw_player_view_level`, which runs as one task per partition. Thread A can save `original_color` after thread B has already installed the black shadow colour; A then "restores" black permanently. Position and the `std::string` are read torn.
3. It is not exception-safe — `TextObject::draw` dereferences a `SpriteFont*` from a map lookup that can be null, and there is no scope guard, so a throw leaves the object permanently black and offset.

Note the const laundering that lets this compile: `draw_countdown_text` is a const member function, but `const` does not propagate through `unique_ptr`, so a non-const `draw` is reachable from it.

Separately, `_shadow_scale` (TextDropShadow.h:38) is independent of `_scale` and `Text::set_scale` does not touch it, so `set_scale(2.0f)` doubles the text and leaves the shadow behind. Level.cpp only avoids this by passing the same constant twice.

```cpp
// TextObject.h:21-23
virtual void draw(DirectX::SpriteBatch* sprite_batch, const MattMath::Camera& camera) const;
virtual void draw(DirectX::SpriteBatch* sprite_batch) const;

// TextDropShadow.h:24-25   -- no trailing const, no override
virtual void draw(DirectX::SpriteBatch* sprite_batc, const MattMath::Camera& camera);
virtual void draw(DirectX::SpriteBatch* sprite_batc);
```
Cv-qualification is part of the member-function signature, so these do not override anything. They start two new virtual slots and name-hide the base pair. Any call through a `Text*`, `TextObject*` or `const TextDropShadow&` dispatches to `TextObject::draw` and renders the text with no drop shadow at all, silently.

It only works today because every use site names the concrete type (`Level::_countdown_text` is `unique_ptr<TextDropShadow>`; the menus call `this->TextDropShadow::draw(...)` explicitly). The first person to store one in a `vector<unique_ptr<Text>>` loses the shadows with no diagnostic.

The missing `override` is exactly why the compiler is silent — `Text.h:21-24` uses `override` correctly on its four setters, `TextDropShadow.h` does not. The parameter is also misspelled `sprite_batc` in both header declarations while the definitions spell it `sprite_batch`, and the header is what shows in IntelliSense.

```cpp
SpriteSheet* SpriteSheetObject::get_sprite_sheet() const {
    return this->get_resource_manager()->get_sprite_sheet(this->get_sprite_sheet_name());
}
```
Each call is an O(log n) red-black-tree walk with full `std::string` compares. `TextureObject::draw` then passes `get_element_name()` into `SpriteSheet::draw`, which does a second string map lookup on `_sprite_frames`.

`AnimationObject` is worse: `get_animation_strip()` (AnimationObject.cpp:24-28) is sheet lookup + strip lookup = two map lookups, and it is called from `get_source_rectangle()` (:139-143) on every draw, from `update()` (:87), from `set_frame_index` (:129) and from `set_frame_time_to_default` (:158). `TextObject::draw` does a font map lookup per string per frame (TextObject.cpp:29-30, 49-50).

Every hop is a virtual call — DrawObject.h:20-25 and SpriteSheetObject.h:19-23 make all the trivial accessors `virtual` — so none of it inlines. Amplification is severe: `draw_player_view_level` redraws every object once per player viewport, and `StructurePaintable::draw` draws its whole `PaintTile` grid, each tile doing a `TextureObject::draw` plus a `PaintTileSplash` `AnimationObject::draw`.

The irony: the dead `AnimatedSprite` class already caches `SpriteSheet*` and `const AnimationStrip*` at construction and never looks a name up again.

```cpp
void AnimationObject::reset() { this->_frame_index = 0; this->_time_elapsed = 0.0f; }
void AnimationObject::stop()  { this->_paused = true; this->reset(); }
void AnimationObject::play()  { this->_paused = false; }
```
`update()` latches `_paused = true` when a non-looping strip reaches its last frame (:102), and `update()` returns immediately while paused (:83-86). `reset()` clears the index and accumulator but not `_paused` — and neither does `set_animation_strip_and_reset` (:144-151), despite the name promising a fresh start.

So once an object plays any non-looping animation to completion, every subsequent `set_animation_strip_and_reset` leaves it stuck on frame 0 of the new strip forever. sprite_sheet_1.json has exactly such a strip (`looping: false` at line 119).

`PaintTileSplash` only escapes because it hand-patches around it: `reset_and_play()` (PaintTile.cpp:176-180) calls `reset()` *and* `play()`. `Player::draw` calls `set_animation_strip_and_reset` with no `play()`, and survives only because all the player strips in the JSON are `looping: true`. Adding one non-looping player animation — a death or hit reaction — freezes the character permanently.

Duplicated verbatim in `AnimatedSprite::reset()` (AnimatedSprite.cpp:85-89).

```cpp
this->_time_elapsed += *this->_dt;
float frame_time = this->_frame_time;
if (this->_time_elapsed > frame_time)   // :90 — single if
{
    this->_frame_index++;
    ...
    this->_time_elapsed -= frame_time;
}
```
At most one frame advances per `update()` no matter how much time accumulated. If `frame_time < dt`, `_time_elapsed` gains `dt - frame_time` every call and grows without bound while the animation caps at one frame per update — it plays slower than authored and the accumulator is permanently poisoned, so `pause()`/`play()` and any later `set_frame_time` behave unpredictably. It also cannot recover from a hitch.

`set_frame_time` (:152-155) validates nothing, and `AnimationStrip::_frame_time` defaults to `0.0f` (AnimationStrip.h:22) with no validation in the JSON decoder (SpriteSheet.cpp:62), so a strip that omits `frame_time` installs zero — at which point `_time_elapsed -= 0` is a no-op and the accumulator diverges forever.

The code is safe today only because the fixed timestep pins dt at 1/60 and every authored frame_time in sprite_sheet_1.json is 0.05-0.2.

I am rejecting one attached sub-claim: a reviewer argued `Player::draw`'s `if (info.frame_time != FLT_MIN)` sentinel (Player.cpp:66) could fail its exact float comparison under /fp:fast and install ~1e-38. /fp:fast is indeed on in all four configs, but both sides of that comparison are the same compile-time constant from player_consts.h with no arithmetic or round-trip between them. The exact-equality-on-float sentinel is still poor style worth replacing, but it is not a live trigger.

```cpp
void DrawObject::set_draw_rotation_by_rectangle_rotated(const RectangleRotated& rect)
{
	// TODO: Implement this function
}
```
A public API on the root of the render hierarchy with an empty body and (grep-confirmed) zero callers. The unnamed-but-declared parameter is unused, and `TreatWarningAsError` is off so nothing surfaces it.

Its intended consumer is `Visual`'s second constructor (Visual.cpp:22-35):
```cpp
this->_rectangle = rect_rotated.get_rectangle_rotated_to_axis();
```
`get_rectangle_rotated_to_axis()` (MattMath.cpp:4456) returns the OBB's extents re-expressed as an axis-aligned box; the orientation is dropped on the floor even though `RectangleRotated::get_angle()` sits four lines below it at MattMath.cpp:4460. The constructor never calls `set_draw_rotation`, so a `Visual` built from a rotated rect renders axis-aligned — a silently wrong picture. The origin is also never set to the rect centre, so even once rotation is applied it would spin about the top-left corner.

Currently latent: `LevelObjectBuilder.cpp:188` and :236 both use the `RectangleF` overload. But `RectangleRotated` was added specifically for the paintable-ramp work in the recent commits, so this constructor is on the critical path for the next feature.

The entire class body is four one-line forwards:
```cpp
void Text::set_text(const std::string& text)      { this->TextObject::set_text(text); }
void Text::set_colour(const Colour& colour)       { this->TextObject::set_colour(colour); }
void Text::set_scale(float scale)                 { this->TextObject::set_scale(scale); }
void Text::set_position(const Vector2F& position) { this->TextObject::set_position(position); }
```
No state, no behaviour. The only semantic effect is that these four are `public` in `Text` (Text.h:21-24) while `protected` in `TextObject`/`DrawObject` (TextObject.h:31-34, DrawObject.h:27) — which is what lets `Level.cpp` call `_countdown_text->set_text(...)`.

The cause is that the mutators are `protected virtual` for no reason other than to make this access-widening override legal, and that virtualness then costs every drawable in the engine. `Text` also adds a layer to an already five-deep chain (`DrawObject` → `TextObject` → `Text` → `TextDropShadow` → `MTextDropShadow`).

(Correcting one reviewer: these are overrides of existing virtuals, so they do not add vtable slots — the cost is the pointless indirection and the extra layer, not extra entries.)

```cpp
class IGameObject {
    virtual void update() = 0;
    virtual void draw(DirectX::SpriteBatch*, const MattMath::Camera&) = 0;
    virtual void draw(DirectX::SpriteBatch*) = 0;
    virtual bool is_visible_in_viewport(const MattMath::RectangleF&) const = 0;
};
```
Four unrelated responsibilities in one interface, so purely static scenery has to implement all four — `Visual::update()` is literally `{ // do nothing }` (Visual.cpp:38-41).

The camera-less `draw` overload exists only so callers can skip the camera, and implementations satisfy it by forwarding with `Camera::DEFAULT_CAMERA` (TextDropShadow.cpp:49-52, Player.cpp:91-95) — a default-argument problem solved with a virtual function, doubling the vtable.

Most importantly for the engine goal, `DirectX::SpriteBatch` appears in the signature, so nothing implementing `IGameObject` can be tested or rendered without DirectXTK. And `draw` being non-const is the root of the whole mutating-draw problem: the level's draw path is `const` (Level.cpp:375, :737) and reaches non-const `draw` only because `const` does not propagate through the `unique_ptr` members.

`DrawObject::_origin` is an untyped `Vector2F` shared by two draw families with different geometry. `TextureObject::draw(batch, RectangleI dest)` forwards it to `SpriteBatch::Draw(texture, RECT destination, RECT const* source, color, rotation, origin, effects, depth)`, where DirectXTK interprets `origin` in **source-texture pixels** and rescales it by the destination/source ratio. The position overload also treats it as texture pixels, multiplied by `scale`.

Callers do not know this. I read `Weapon::draw` (Weapon.cpp:36-66):
```cpp
auto draw_rectangle = RectangleF(draw_pos, this->get_details().size);
Vector2F origin = this->calculate_sprite_origin(this->get_details().size, rotation_origin::LEFT_CENTER);
...
DrawObject::set_origin(origin);
TextureObject::draw(sprite_batch, draw_rectangle, camera);
```
`get_details().size` is world units, not atlas pixels, so the pivot is wrong by the world-to-texel ratio — correct only by coincidence when the two happen to match. Same pattern in WeaponRoller.cpp.

The camera-aware overloads compound it: `TextureObject::draw(..., const Camera&)` transforms the destination rectangle (TextureObject.cpp:59-60) but leaves `_origin` untouched, so the two end up in different spaces.

The root problem is design, not arithmetic: a bare `Vector2F` on a base class, set through an untyped setter, with the unit convention documented nowhere.

```cpp
const static MattMath::Vector2F TOP_RIGHT_BOX_SIZE = { 0.15f, 0.07f };
const static std::string TIMER_FONT = "gill_sans_mt_bold_48";
const static MattMath::Colour HEALTH_COLOUR = colour_consts::GREEN;
```
Same in `debug_text_consts` (DebugText.h:11-18).

At namespace scope `const` already implies internal linkage, so `static` is redundant (and written backwards — the idiom is `static const`). The real cost is that `std::string`, `Colour` and `Vector2F` are not literal types here, so each gets a separate object *and* a dynamic initialiser in every TU that includes the header — and both headers are pulled in via `Level.h`. The file mixes `constexpr` and `const static` for values of the same kind on adjacent lines (`TIMER_SCALE_FACTOR` vs `TIMER_SHADOW_OFFSET`), so the inconsistency is visible at a glance.

Everything textured funnels through:
```cpp
void TextureObject::draw(SpriteBatch* sprite_batch, const RectangleF& destination_rectangle) const
{
	this->draw(sprite_batch, destination_rectangle.get_rectangle_i());
}
```
`RectangleF::get_rectangle_i()` (MattMath.cpp:823-829) is four independent `static_cast<int>` — truncation toward zero, not floor — and `RectangleI::get_win_rect()` (MattMath.cpp:2043-2052) then reconstitutes edges as `right = x + width`. Truncating origin and extent separately is the classic tiling defect: two abutting tiles at `x=10.6,w=4.5` and `x=15.1,w=4.5` become `[10,14]` and `[15,19]`, a 1-pixel gap. And because truncation is toward zero rather than floor, the mapping is not uniform across x=0, so a sprite scrolling left across the origin jumps a pixel.

Same pattern at AnimationObject.cpp:44-48, Drawer.cpp:42-53, and the four HUD rects in InterfaceGameplay.cpp.

I am grading this low rather than medium: the reviewers asserted visible shimmering seams across every painted surface, which depends on the paint grid producing fractional edges and which I did not verify. The rounding defect itself is unambiguous.

findings.suggestion:
Round the *edges*, not the position and size, so abutting tiles share an edge whenever their float edges coincide:
```cpp
inline RECT to_pixel_rect(const RectangleF& r) {
    return { std::lround(r.x), std::lround(r.y), std::lround(r.x + r.width), std::lround(r.y + r.height) };
}
```
Put it on `RectangleF` and route every destination-rect conversion through it. Alternatively use the float position + scale form of `SpriteBatch::Draw` and preserve sub-pixel motion entirely.

findings.detail:
```cpp
switch (origin)
{
case rotation_origin::CENTER:      return Vector2F(size) / 2.0f;
case rotation_origin::LEFT_CENTER: return { 0.0f, size.y / 2.0f };
default: // rotation_origin::TOP_LEFT
    return Vector2F::ZERO;
}
```
`rotation_origin` has four enumerators — `CENTER, TOP_LEFT, LEFT_CENTER, NO_ROTATION` (rotation_origin.h:4-10). The `default:` swallows both `TOP_LEFT` and `NO_ROTATION` while the comment claims only `TOP_LEFT`, and because `default:` is present MSVC emits no C4061/C4062, so the unhandled enumerator is invisible under /W4. `NO_ROTATION` means "do not rotate this sprite", which an origin function cannot express at all; it is being answered with a pivot point.

And the enumerator is not theoretical: `projectile_details.h:11` declares `rotation_origin rotat_origin = rotation_origin::NO_ROTATION;` and `projectile_consts.h` sets it on five of six projectile types (lines 18, 31, 65, 78, 99). A grep across every .cpp finds **zero reads** of `rotat_origin` — the field is written by the content tables and never consumed, so all projectile sprite origins silently fall back to `DrawObject::_origin`'s default of ZERO.

`Weapon::calculate_sprite_origin` (Weapon.cpp:88-101) spells out `TOP_LEFT` explicitly and then adds a `default: return Vector2F::ZERO;` — same silent fold, arrived at by copy-paste drift.

```
ID3D11DeviceContext*
DeviceResources::get_deferred_context(int index) const noexcept
{
    if (index < 0 || index >= this->_deferred_contexts->size())
    {
        throw std::out_of_range("Invalid index.");
    }
    return this->_deferred_contexts->at(index);
}
```
Declaration at DeviceResources.h:88 is also `noexcept`. Throwing from `noexcept` calls `std::terminate` — no unwinding, no message, the one defensive path in the function hard-kills the process. Three more defects in the same three lines: (a) `index >= ...->size()` compares `int` to `size_type` (C4018 at /W4, and `TreatWarningAsError` is not set anywhere in the vcxproj); (b) `_deferred_contexts` is dereferenced to call `size()` before any null check, so it is a null deref if called before `create_deferred_contexts`; (c) `at()` re-does the bounds check that was just done by hand, so the manual check buys nothing but the terminate.

It is reachable: `MenuPage::draw_range_of_mobjects_in_viewports` (MenuPage.cpp:172-177) indexes the pool by **menu-object index**, not thread index — `get_deferred_context(i)` and `command_lists->at(i)` where `i` runs over the mobject list, while `command_lists` is sized to `deferred_contexts->size()` (MenuPage.cpp:128). Any menu page with more than 16 drawable widgets terminates the process here.

```
void Game::OnDeviceLost()
{
    for (auto& sprite_batch : this->_sprite_batches) { sprite_batch.reset(); }
    _states.reset();
    this->_resource_manager->reset_all_textures();
    ...
}
```
It resets the 16 owning `unique_ptr<SpriteBatch>`s but never touches `_sprite_batches_ptrs` (Game.h:73), the hand-maintained parallel `std::vector<SpriteBatch*>` whose **address** was published to GameData at Game.cpp:234 and which every draw path dereferences (GameStates.cpp:317, MenuPage.cpp:129, Level.cpp:513). Same for `_states.reset()`: `GameData::_common_states` (GameData.h:58) still points at the freed `CommonStates`, and `Level::_sampler_state` (Level.h:141) still holds the `ID3D11SamplerState*` that CommonStates owned and has now released. `this->_resource_manager->reset_all_textures()` is also unguarded against a null `_resource_manager`.

The lost/restored pair is asymmetric: `OnDeviceLost` clears three things, `OnDeviceRestored` recreates ten.

```
// Add a 4th viewport if there are 3 players
if (player_count == 3)
{
    result.push_back(this->get_player_viewport(4));
}
```
Valid indices are 0..3. Traced with `_layout == THREE_PLAYER`: index 4 misses the THREE_PLAYER inner switch (`default: break` at :205), falls into FOUR_PLAYER, misses again (:235), and lands in the outer `default:` at :238 — a **fullscreen** viewport. THREE_PLAYER assigns 0=top-left, 1=bottom-right, 2=bottom-left, so the intended fill is the empty top-right quadrant.

`MenuPage::draw_mobject_in_viewports` (MenuPage.cpp:99-115) iterates the returned vector: for `i == 3` it calls `apply_player_viewport(3, …)` — which under THREE_PLAYER falls through to FOUR_PLAYER case 3 = **bottom-right**, a duplicate of player 1's viewport — and then draws the widget positioned against `viewports[3]`, which is the **fullscreen** rect. The two disagree, so in a 3-player menu the fourth pass lays the widget out for a full screen while scissoring it to the bottom-right quadrant, and the actually-blank top-right quadrant is never drawn into.

```
void ViewportManager::apply_player_viewport(int player_num) const
{
    D3D11_VIEWPORT vp = ...;
    auto context = this->_device_resources->GetD3DDeviceContext();  // IMMEDIATE context
    context->RSSetViewports(1, &vp);
    this->_sprite_batch->SetViewport(vp);                            // SpriteBatch #0, bound to DEFERRED context 0
}
```
`_sprite_batch` is wired at construction to `get_sprite_batches()->at(0)` (Game.cpp:247-249), which was built on deferred context 0 (Game.cpp:229). The two halves of one call target two different contexts, so the `RSSetViewports` is not part of the command list being recorded — the deferred context keeps whatever viewport `Game::clear()` last set.

The only live caller is `Level::draw_zoom_out_level_component` (Level.cpp:620), which runs **inside a thread-pool task** (Level.cpp:601-606). `ID3D11DeviceContext` is not free-threaded and the immediate context must never be touched from a worker while the main thread is in `clear()`/`Present()`. The same task also does `this->_viewport_manager->set_layout(screen_layout::ONE_PLAYER)` (Level.cpp:619) — mutating shared manager state from a worker and never restoring it. It only appears to work because the forced ONE_PLAYER viewport happens to equal the fullscreen one.

`Game(Game&&) = default; Game& operator= (Game&&) = default;` — meanwhile `Game` hands out three pointers into itself:
- `Game::Game()` calls `_device_resources->RegisterDeviceNotify(this)` (Game.cpp:20), stored as `IDeviceNotify* m_deviceNotify` (DeviceResources.h:126, whose comment 'can be held directly as it owns the DeviceResources' is only true if the owner never moves);
- `create_device_dependent_resources` publishes `&this->_sprite_batches_ptrs` — the address of a member vector — into GameData (Game.cpp:234);
- `StateContext::transition_to` stores `this` into the State, and `Game` is the root StateContext (Game.h:16).

Moving a `Game` leaves all three pointing at the moved-from husk. The user-declared destructor at Game.h:20 would already have suppressed the implicit move, so these two lines were added deliberately and are pure hazard — `Game` is only ever held in a `unique_ptr` (Main.cpp:20), so nothing needs them.

```
if (!_audio_engine->Update())
{
    // more about this below...
}
```
The DirectXTK template's placeholder comment, never filled in. `AudioEngine::Update()` returns false when the audio graph has stopped — either a lost device or no audio device at all. The documented handling is `if (_audio_engine->IsCriticalError()) { _audio_engine->Reset(); }` to re-acquire the new default endpoint. As written, unplugging headphones or switching output device silently mutes the game for the rest of the session while `Update()` keeps being called 60 times a second on a dead engine, and the empty `if` body is compiled away.

```
std::unique_ptr<Game> g_game;
std::unique_ptr<Save> g_save;
std::unique_ptr<ResolutionManager> g_resolution_manager;
std::unique_ptr<GameData> g_game_data;
std::unique_ptr<SimpleMath::Vector2> g_screen_resolution;
```
Statics destruct in reverse construction order, i.e. `g_screen_resolution`, `g_game_data`, `g_resolution_manager`, `g_save`, then `g_game` **last** — and `g_game` is the one object that transitively depends on all the others (it holds `GameData* _data`, which holds `Save*` and `ResolutionManager*` per GameData.h:49-50, and owns the whole State tree).

The only reason this does not blow up is the manual `g_game.reset();` at Main.cpp:226, which is skipped on both early-return paths (`return 1` at :126 and :185) and on any exception escaping the loop. `CoUninitialize()` at :228 is likewise skipped on those paths, and on the normal path `g_game_data`/`g_save`/`g_resolution_manager` are destroyed during static destruction, i.e. after the COM apartment has already been torn down. The correctness of the shutdown sequence rests on one undocumented line.

```
Viewport vp = this->get_player_viewport(player_num);
const float scale = camera.scale;
auto result = RectangleF(translation.x, translation.y, vp.width * scale, vp.height * scale);
```
The result is used as a WORLD-space culling rect: `Level::draw_player_view_level` feeds it into `object->is_visible_in_viewport(camera_view)` at Level.cpp:518, 527, 536.

`Camera::scale` is world→screen: `calculate_view_rectangle` computes `(world - translation) * scale` (MattMath.cpp:4119-4122) and `calculate_camera_from_view_rectangle` sets `scale = world_rectangle.width / view_rectangle.width` (MattMath.cpp:4167). The world extent covered by a `vp.width`-pixel viewport is therefore `vp.width / scale`, not `vp.width * scale`, and the error compounds to a factor of scale-squared.

Currently latent: every camera reaching this function comes from `CameraTools::calculate_camera`, which only ever adjusts `translation` and leaves `scale` at 1.0, and the zoom-out path (Level.cpp:150-159) does not use this function. Any zoom feature — or any reuse of ViewportManager in another game, which is the stated goal — makes objects get culled exactly when they should become visible.

```
namespace viewport_consts
{
    constexpr float DIVIDER_THICKNESS = 2.0f;
    const std::string DIVIDER_SHEET_NAME = "sprite_sheet_1";
    const std::string DIVIDER_FRAME_NAME = "pixel";
    const MattMath::Colour DIVIDER_COLOUR = colour_consts::BLACK;
}
```
Two problems. (1) A viewport/display service has no business knowing which sprite sheet a divider is skinned from — these are consumed by `LevelObjectBuilder::build_viewport_dividers`, and they force ViewportManager.h to include Colour.h. (2) Namespace-scope `const` objects have internal linkage, so every TU that includes this header gets its own copy with its own dynamic initialiser (two heap-allocating std::strings plus a Colour constructor at static-init time). ViewportManager.h is included by GameData.h:9, which almost every file in the project includes — dozens of duplicate objects and static initialisers for three constants used at one site.

```
class com_exception : public std::exception
{
public:
    com_exception(HRESULT hr) noexcept : result(hr) {}
    const char* what() const noexcept override
    {
        static char s_str[64] = {};
        sprintf_s(s_str, "Failure with HRESULT of %08X", ...);
        return s_str;
    }
```
Every `com_exception` in the process returns a pointer to the same 64-byte static. Calling `what()` on exception A after exception B overwrites A's message, and the returned pointer's lifetime is not tied to the exception object — the opposite of what callers expect. The single-argument constructor is not `explicit`, so any HRESULT implicitly converts.

This is verbatim Microsoft template code but it lives in the project's own pch.h and is the only exception type here carrying useful information — the rest of the codebase throws `std::exception("literal")`, an MSVC-only extension.

```
enum class screen_resolution
{
    S_1280_720, S_1920_1080, S_2560_1440, S_3840_2160,
    MAX = S_3840_2160
};
```
`MAX` is an alias for a valid value, and `MainMenu.cpp:572` relies on that (`int enum_max = static_cast<int>(screen_resolution::MAX);` then wraps inclusively). Adding a fifth resolution *after* MAX silently drops it from the menu cycle; adding it before silently changes MAX's meaning. There is also no fixed underlying type, and the enumerator names encode pixel dimensions that ResolutionManager.cpp:47-63 and :152-175 then hard-code a second and third time.

Merged from 4 near-identical candidates. Verbatim at SoundBank.cpp:152-161:

    FILE* fp = fopen(json_path, "rb");
    auto read_buffer = std::make_unique<char>();
    FileReadStream is(fp, read_buffer.get(), sizeof(read_buffer));
    ...
    fclose(fp);
    read_buffer.release();

Three compounded defects, all verified:
1. `std::make_unique<char>()` allocates exactly ONE char. `std::make_unique<char[]>(N)` was intended.
2. `sizeof(read_buffer)` is `sizeof(std::unique_ptr<char>)` == 8 on x64 (4 on Win32), not the buffer size. I read external/rapidjson/include/rapidjson/filereadstream.h: the ctor asserts `bufferSize >= 4` (line 46, passes), then `Read()` does `readCount_ = std::fread(buffer_, 1, bufferSize_, fp_)` (line 71) — 8 bytes into a 1-byte heap block, repeatedly for the whole file — and on the final short read also writes `buffer_[readCount_] = '\0'` (line 76), index up to 7. Heap corruption on every asset load.
3. `read_buffer.release()` relinquishes ownership WITHOUT deleting, so the block leaks. The author likely meant `reset()`, or nothing at all.

All four sites verified line by line: ArtAttack/SoundBank.cpp:152-161, ArtAttack/Save.cpp:14-23, ArtAttack/SpriteSheet.cpp:72-81, ArtAttack/LevelLoadedInfo.cpp:96-110 (the last uses the name `readBuffer`). Secondary effect: the file is consumed 8 bytes at a time, so a 22 KB level JSON costs ~2,700 fread calls.

Merged from 5 candidates (all five reviewers found this). Verbatim:

    ID3D11ShaderResourceView* ResourceManager::get_texture(const std::string& texture_name)
    {
        try { return this->_textures[texture_name].Get(); }
        catch (const std::out_of_range&) { throw std::out_of_range("Texture " + texture_name + " not found."); }
    }

`std::map::operator[]` never throws `out_of_range` — it default-inserts. So:
- All four catch blocks are unreachable dead code: get_texture (7-19), get_sprite_font (27-38), get_sprite_sheet (46-58), get_sound_bank (91-102). The "not found" diagnostic the author believes they wrote can never run.
- A misspelled or unloaded name silently grows the map with a null entry and returns `nullptr`, which callers dereference unchecked far away: `SoundBankObject::get_sb()->play_wave(...)` (SoundBankObject.cpp:25) and `SpriteSheetObject::get_sprite_sheet()` (SpriteSheetObject.cpp:31-35) feeding `SpriteBatch::Draw`.
- The lookup MUTATES the container, which is why all four are non-const, which forces every consumer to hold a mutable `ResourceManager*`.

`get_level_info` (66-78) is the counterexample in the same file: it uses `.at()`, it is the only one that reports missing keys, and it is the only one that is `const`. The correct idiom is already present ten lines away.

Same defect one level down in `SpriteSheet::draw`: `this->_sprite_frames[frame_name]` at SpriteSheet.cpp:105 and :126 silently inserts a zero-initialised SpriteFrame for an unknown frame name.

Merged from 5 candidates.

    void ResourceManager::reset_all_sprite_fonts() { for (auto& font : _sprite_fonts) font.second.reset(); }
    void ResourceManager::reset_all_textures()     { for (auto& t : _textures) t.second.Reset(); }
    void ResourceManager::reset_all_sounds()       { for (auto& sb : _sound_banks) { sb.second->reset_all_instances(); sb.second.reset(); } }

Every key survives with a null value. Combined with the `operator[]`/`.at()`+`.get()` getters above, a post-reset lookup SUCCEEDS and returns `nullptr`, so failure surfaces as an access violation inside `SpriteBatch::Draw` or `SoundEffectInstance::Play` instead of at the lookup. `SoundBank::reset_all_instances` (SoundBank.cpp:180-187) has the identical shape.

Three further verified problems in these 22 lines, all reachable from `Game::OnDeviceLost` (Game.cpp:269-280):
- `reset_all_sounds()` destroys the SoundBank objects outright, but `Level::_sound_bank` is a raw non-owning pointer cached once at Level.cpp:59 and used at Level.cpp:63, 73, 74, 134, 136, 147, 171. Those are dangling immediately, and Level is never notified.
- Audio has no relationship to the D3D11 device. Tearing down every wave bank and voice because the GPU was lost is simply wrong.
- `sb.second->reset_all_instances()` is redundant — the very next statement destroys the whole SoundBank, which destroys its instances anyway.
- The API is asymmetric: `_sprite_sheets` and `_level_infos` have no reset, so SpriteSheet objects survive holding the raw `ID3D11ShaderResourceView*` that `reset_all_textures` just released.

Merged from 4 candidates.

    void CALLBACK ThreadPool::work_callback(PTP_CALLBACK_INSTANCE instance, PVOID parameter, PTP_WORK work)
    {
        std::function<void()>* task = static_cast<std::function<void()>*>(parameter);
        (*task)();
        delete task;
    }

An exception escaping `(*task)()` unwinds out of a Win32 thread-pool callback — undefined behaviour, in practice `std::terminate` on a worker thread mid-frame with no diagnostic — and `delete task` is skipped, so the payload leaks.

This is reachable, not hypothetical. I verified the submitted tasks throw: `Level::draw_zoom_out_level` submits a task at Level.cpp:601 whose body reaches `throw std::exception("Failed to finish command list")` at Level.cpp:590; `MenuPage::draw_mobjects_in_viewports` submits at MenuPage.cpp:142 and its body throws the same at MenuPage.cpp:120. Add the `std::out_of_range` throws in ResourceManager/SoundBank that also run on that path.

`instance` and `work` are both unused named parameters — C4100 at /W4.

findings.suggestion:
Adopt ownership first, then catch at the boundary:

    std::unique_ptr<std::function<void()>> owner(static_cast<std::function<void()>*>(parameter));
    try { (*owner)(); } catch (...) { /* store exception_ptr on the pool */ }

and rethrow the first captured exception from `wait_for_tasks_to_complete()` so the failure lands on the submitting thread with its message intact.

findings.detail:
Merged from 3 candidates. This is the sharpest engine-reuse blocker in the module.

    void ResourceLoader::load_textures() const { load_sprite_sheet_from_directory(TEXTURE_DIRECTORY, "sprite_sheet_1"); }
    void ResourceLoader::load_fonts() { load_sprite_font_from_directory(FONT_DIRECTORY, "gill_sans_ultra_bold_144"); ...seven names... }
    void ResourceLoader::load_sounds() { load_sound_bank_from_directory(SOUNDS_DIRECTORY, "sound_bank_1"); }
    void ResourceLoader::load_level_info() const
    {
        load_level_info_from_directory(LEVEL_DIRECTORY, "king_of_the_hill", level_stage::KING_OF_THE_HILL);
        load_level_info_from_directory(LEVEL_DIRECTORY, "turbulence",       level_stage::TURBULENCE);
        load_level_info_from_directory(LEVEL_DIRECTORY, "close_quarters",   level_stage::CLOSE_QUARTERS);
    }

`load_all_resources()` (45-51) is the only externally-called entry point in the class, and it is a hardcoded list of twelve Splatoon-clone assets. Shipping a second game means forking this file. The loader is also the only place that knows the asset-file-to-lookup-key association, so nothing outside it can register an asset without a code change. Extensions are hardcoded too (".dds", ".spritefont", ".xwb", ".json" at lines 79, 107, 131-132, 148, 172-173).

It also loads serially on the main thread — the ThreadPool is constructed 22 lines earlier at Game.cpp:236 and sits idle — with no try/catch anywhere, so the first missing file terminates startup, and only `load_sprite_font` (83-101) adds any context to the error at all.

Merged from 4 candidates.

    PTP_WORK work = CreateThreadpoolWork(work_callback,
        new std::function<void()>(task), &_callback_environment);
    if (!work) { throw std::runtime_error("Failed to create thread pool work object."); }
    _work_items.push_back(work);
    SubmitThreadpoolWork(work);

The `new`'d function is unowned between the allocation and the eventual callback. On the `!work` path the throw orphans it permanently — nothing will run `work_callback` to delete it. If `_work_items.push_back` throws `bad_alloc`, the work object is never submitted and never closed either.

Separately the task is copied twice: `std::function<void()> task` is a by-value parameter (copy 1), then copy-constructed into the heap object because `task` is named rather than moved (copy 2). The Level.cpp:388 lambda captures a whole `std::vector<std::pair<int,int>>` by value, so this is not free.

I graded medium rather than high: the leak needs an allocation failure, whereas the callback finding above fires on ordinary draw errors.

findings.suggestion:
auto payload = std::make_unique<std::function<void()>>(std::move(task));
PTP_WORK work = CreateThreadpoolWork(work_callback, payload.get(), &_callback_environment);
if (!work) throw std::runtime_error("...");
_work_items.push_back(work);   // may throw; still owned
SubmitThreadpoolWork(work);
payload.release();             // ownership transfers only now

findings.detail:
Merged from 3 candidates.

    void Save::load_save_file()
    {
        if (check_if_save_file_exists()) { std::cout << "Save file exists.\n"; }
        else { std::cout << "Save file does not exist.\n"; this->save_to_file(); }
        this->_save_data = this->load_from_json(SAVE_FILE_PATH.c_str());  // unconditional
    }

`write_save_file` (58-83) correctly returns `false` when `fopen(SAVE_FILE_PATH, "wb")` fails — but `save_to_file` (85-99) is `void` and consumes that bool only to choose which of two `std::cout` messages to print, then returns. Execution falls straight through to `load_from_json` on a file that was just proved absent, which is the unchecked-fopen path from the earlier finding: `fread(buf, 1, 8, nullptr)` and `fclose(nullptr)` in Release.

Reachable in the ordinary case: `directory_consts.h:11` is the CWD-relative `"./save/save_data.json"`, fopen does not create directories, and there is no post-build asset copy step (I grepped the vcxproj — no PostBuildEvent, no Copy/xcopy). This is the very first thing the app does.

One candidate called it "guaranteed access violation on first launch outside the VS debugger" — that overstates it slightly (it depends on the CWD lacking a ./save/ directory), so I downgraded from high to medium.

Merged from 4 candidates — all four found it, and it is exactly as described.

    auto legacy_start = std::chrono::system_clock::to_time_t(start);
    char buffer[30];
    std::cout << ctime_s(buffer, sizeof(buffer), &legacy_start) << " Saving...\n";

`ctime_s` has signature `errno_t ctime_s(char*, size_t, const time_t*)` — it returns an error code and writes the text into `buffer`. This prints `0 Saving...`. `buffer`, the thing that actually holds the timestamp the whole `chrono` dance was set up to produce, is written and never read. The return value is also the only failure signal ctime_s gives, and it is being swallowed into a stream insertion. `30` is a magic number (the documented minimum is 26).

Worth noting the whole logging block is invisible anyway: Main.cpp creates a GUI app with no console, so these `std::cout` diagnostics (Save.cpp:47, 51, 93, 97) go nowhere.

    void Weapon::handle_shoot_sound(bool shooting_this_update, bool holding_shoot)
    {
        const std::string& sound_name = this->get_sound_effect_instance_name();
        if (holding_shoot || shooting_this_update)
            this->_sound_bank->play_effect(sound_name, true, this->_details.shoot_sound_volume);
        else
            this->_sound_bank->stop_effect(sound_name, true);
    }

Called unconditionally from `Weapon::update` (Weapon.cpp:231) — every tick, for every weapon. `SoundBank::play_effect` (SoundBank.cpp:29-38) then does `_sound_effect_instances.at(name)` over 48 entries, followed by SetVolume/SetPitch/SetPan/Play. None of the three level values change between frames, and re-calling Play on an already-looping instance is pure overhead; the else-branch calls Stop(true) on an already-stopped instance 60 times a second.

Same shape at Level.cpp:171: `stop_effect(this->_music_name, true)` runs on every update while the level sits in `level_state::FINISHED`.

One candidate also claimed Level.cpp:73 plays music every frame — that one is WRONG; it is guarded by a first-update check at Level.cpp:71 (`_start_timer >= START_TIMER`). Corrected here.

Merged from 3 candidates, with one sub-claim corrected.

    namespace directory_consts {
        const static std::string TEXTURE_DIRECTORY = "./textures/";
        const static std::string LEVEL_DIRECTORY = "./levels/";
        const static std::string FONT_DIRECTORY = "./fonts/";
        const static std::string SAVE_FILE_PATH = "./save/save_data.json";
        const static std::string SOUNDS_DIRECTORY = "./sounds/";
    }

1. These resolve against the process CWD, not the executable location. I grepped ArtAttack.vcxproj for PostBuildEvent/Copy/xcopy — no matches — and the asset folders sit next to the .vcxproj, not the built exe. The app therefore only finds its assets when launched by the VS debugger (CWD defaults to $(ProjectDir)). Double-clicking the built exe hits the unchecked-fopen crash path.
2. `SAVE_FILE_PATH` writes settings into the install directory, which fails on any properly-installed app; and `write_save_file` returning false is swallowed (Save.cpp:91-98) so settings silently do not persist. It is also a FILE living in a namespace called `directory_consts`.
3. `const` at namespace scope already gives internal linkage, so `static` is redundant. Each including TU still gets its own five `std::string` objects, dynamically initialised before main. CORRECTION to one candidate: it claimed all five exceed MSVC's 15-char SSO limit "except ./levels/". That is wrong — "./textures/" (11), "./fonts/" (8), "./levels/" (9) and "./sounds/" (9) all fit in SSO; only SAVE_FILE_PATH (21 chars) heap-allocates. The dynamic-initialisation-per-TU point stands regardless.
4. The layout is a policy decision frozen into an engine header, compounded by ResourceLoader's hardcoded extensions.

    try { this->_resource_manager->add_sprite_font(font_name, std::make_unique<SpriteFont>(this->_device, ...)); }
    catch (const std::exception&)
    {
