```cpp
void StateContext::transition_to(std::unique_ptr<State> state)
{
    this->_state = std::move(state);   // runs ~State() on the CURRENT state
    this->_state->set_context(this);
    this->_state->init();
}
```
`unique_ptr::operator=(unique_ptr&&)` destroys the previously-managed object. Every non-bootstrap transition in the codebase is issued by the state being destroyed, from inside its own `update()`. Grep found 45 `transition_to` occurrences across 6 files; the self-destroying ones are GameStates.cpp:81 (`GameMenu::order_level_creation`, reached from `GameMenu::update` at :76), GameStates.cpp:211, 252, 262, 272, 290 (inside `GameLevel::update`), 21 sites in MainMenu.cpp, and 4 in PauseMenu.cpp (:70, :77, :225, :250). Only Main.cpp:208 is a legitimate outside-in transition.

So the effective semantics are `delete this;` mid-member-function. Two consequences: (a) `this->_state->init()` — which for `GameLevel` builds an entire level, loads textures and sounds — runs nested inside the destroyed `GameMenu`'s still-live stack frame; (b) any statement after the call touches freed memory.

I checked every call site and none currently reads a member afterwards, so it does not crash today. That is luck, not design: adding one log line, one `this->play_wave(...)`, or one member reset after any of the ~30 calls is an immediate heap-use-after-free. GameStates.cpp:252-254 doesn't even `return` — it breaks out of two nested switches and falls off the end of `GameLevel::update`.

Severity is `high` rather than `critical` only because no live path currently dereferences the freed object.

findings.suggestion:
Make transitions deferred: `request_transition(std::unique_ptr<State>)` parks the new state in a `_pending` member, and `StateContext::update()` applies it *after* `_state->update()` returns:
```cpp
void StateContext::update() {
    if (_state) _state->update();
    if (_pending) { _state = std::move(_pending); _state->set_context(this); _state->init(); }
}
```
No state is then ever destroyed while a frame of it is live, and call sites can fall through naturally instead of every one having to remember to return.

Construct fully before publishing, which fixes both at once:
```cpp
if (!state) { _state.reset(); return; }
state->set_context(this);
state->init();
_state = std::move(state);
```
On throw the old state is still installed and the parameter's own unique_ptr destroys the half-built one — a strong exception guarantee. Better still, move init() work into constructors so two-phase init disappears.

findings.detail:
Because the interface asks each object "are you colliding with this other object?", the same four-step algorithm (type filter → AABB test → shape test) is written out separately in Player.cpp:148-185, PaintTile.cpp:84-109, ProjectileSpray.cpp:56, ProjectileJet.cpp:54, ProjectileBall.cpp:55, ProjectileMist.cpp:56 and ProjectileRolling.cpp:48.

Worse, `Structure::is_colliding` (Structure.cpp:43-46) is:
```cpp
bool Structure::is_colliding(const ICollisionGameObject* other) const
{
	return false;
}
```
So the answer to "are these two colliding" depends on which one you ask. `Level::update_level_logic` therefore has to ask both orderings and dispatch `on_collision` to each participant twice per frame (Level.cpp:222-293, three overlapping loop nests with the same pairs tested twice and only an `object == object_2` self-check).

Every cheap rejection also costs 5+ virtual calls, because everything a pair needs (`get_for_deletion`, `get_collision_object_type`, `get_shape`, and `get_bounding_box` inside the shape test) is behind a pure virtual. There is no broad phase at all, and the `ThreadPool`/`Partitioner` the project already owns are used only for drawing (Level.cpp:379-400) — collision is single-threaded and unaccelerated over a list that grows with every projectile fired (Level.cpp:204-210).

```cpp
virtual void draw(DirectX::SpriteBatch* sprite_batch, const MattMath::Camera& camera) = 0;
virtual void draw(DirectX::SpriteBatch* sprite_batch) = 0;
```
This is the base interface every drawable derives from — gameplay objects, projectiles, paint tiles, and the whole `Mh` UI widget tree. The rendering backend is therefore part of the type identity of every object in the engine, and `IGameObject.h:4` includes `MattMath.h` (1139 lines, itself pulling `SimpleMath.h` and `<d3d11.h>`) just to name them. Changing renderer, changing batching strategy, or introducing a render-command queue means editing all ~30 implementers.

The two-overload requirement compounds it: every implementer must write both, and the camera-less one is pure boilerplate for game objects — `Player::draw(SpriteBatch*)` (Player.cpp:91-95) just forwards with `Camera::DEFAULT_CAMERA`, as do ProjectileBall/Jet/Spray/Mist/Rolling and Structure. It exists only so UI widgets, which have no camera, can be drawn. The level renderer only ever calls the camera form (Level.cpp:520, 529, 538, 550).

A `State` — an object whose job is "which screen am I on" — reaches through the service locator for deferred contexts, allocates a raw `std::vector<ID3D11CommandList*>`, hands it to `Level::draw` as an out-param, then fetches the immediate context and hand-loops `ExecuteCommandList` / `Release`:
```cpp
std::vector<ID3D11CommandList*> command_lists(deferred_contexts->size(), nullptr);
...
for (int i = 0; i < command_lists.size(); i++) {
    if (command_lists[i] == nullptr) continue;
    immediate_context->ExecuteCommandList(command_lists[i], TRUE);
    command_lists[i]->Release();
}
```
This is the only place in the codebase where command lists are executed, so any other state wanting multithreaded drawing must copy this block verbatim, and swapping D3D11 means editing the state machine.

Four concrete defects in the same block:
- **Ownership crosses a thread boundary as a raw pointer.** The references are created with refcount 1 by `FinishCommandList` on thread-pool workers (Level.cpp:587, 646) and released by hand here. Nothing in `Level::draw`'s signature communicates the handover.
- **Signed/unsigned compare** at :321 (`int i < command_lists.size()`) — C4018 under the project's /W4, accumulating silently since `TreatWarningAsError` is set in neither project. The sibling `check_for_pause_input` (:351) casts correctly, so the file disagrees with itself.
- **Per-frame heap allocation, 4× oversized.** `deferred_contexts->size()` is `NUM_THREADS_MAX` = 16 (Game.h:14, Game.cpp:52), but `Partitioner::partition` caps its loop at `i < num_elements` (Partitioner.cpp:10) and it is partitioning `_player_objects->size()` (max 4, Level.cpp:383). Slots 4-15 are always null. 16 deferred contexts and 16 SpriteBatches exist to service at most 4 recorders.
- **`ExecuteCommandList(..., TRUE)`** forces a full save/restore of the immediate-context state once per player per frame; the paired `FinishCommandList(TRUE, ...)` at Level.cpp:587/646 does the same.
- `get_deferred_contexts()` is fetched twice (:311 and :315) for the same value.

```cpp
case game_level_state::FIRST_UPDATE:
    this->_state = game_level_state::SECOND_UPDATE;
    return;
case game_level_state::SECOND_UPDATE:
    this->_state = game_level_state::ACTIVE;
    //no break here so it goes to active
```
with `draw()` early-returning for both states (:305-309). No comment explains what the two dead updates are waiting for; the evident intent is to swallow the huge first frame delta after `init()` loads a level. It does not work, for three reasons:

1. `Game::tick` uses a fixed timestep (`_timer.SetFixedTimeStep(true)`, Game.cpp:64) and `StepTimer::Tick` runs `while (m_leftOverTicks >= m_targetElapsedTicks) { ...; update(); }` (StepTimer.h:132-141) with the raw delta clamped to `m_qpcMaxDelta` (StepTimer.h:104-108, default 1/10s) — up to 6 `update()` calls per single `tick()` at a 1/60 target. After a slow `init()`, FIRST_UPDATE → SECOND_UPDATE → ACTIVE all fire inside one tick and the first real `_level->update()` still runs against the backlog.
2. Under fixed timestep `*this->_dt = float(timer.GetElapsedSeconds())` (Game.cpp:89-90) is always the fixed step anyway, so the large-delta problem the hack guards against cannot reach the simulation through `_dt` in the first place.
3. Despite two states, only *one* update is actually skipped — SECOND_UPDATE deliberately falls through into ACTIVE.

Meanwhile `draw()` returning early means `Game::render` clears and presents nothing: a black flash on every level start and on every RESTART, since `_state` is reset to FIRST_UPDATE at :204 and :281.

```cpp
Vector2F MovingObject::get_unit_velocity() const { return this->_velocity.normalized(); }
Vector2F MovingObject::get_unit_dx() const       { return this->_dx.normalized(); }
```
`Vector2F::normalized()` (MattMath.cpp:1724-1728) has no zero guard:
```cpp
float length = this->length();
return Vector2F(this->x / length, this->y / length);
```
Both members default to `Vector2F::ZERO` (MovingObject.h:54-55), so calling either on an idle object yields `(NaN, NaN)`.

**Six reviewers overstated the consequence.** I grepped the whole tree: `get_unit_velocity`, `get_unit_dx`, `get_velocity_angle` and `get_dx_angle` have **zero callers** outside their own definitions. Nothing consumes the NaN, so the claims that it "silently poisons positions, collision directions and camera targets" are false. Downgraded to `low`: this is latent dead code, and the real defect is one layer down in `MattMath::Vector2F::normalized()`/`normalize()`, which other code does call (PlayerInput.cpp:99 guards it with a deadzone check; RectangleRotated::set_x_axis, MattMath.cpp:4361, validates afterwards).

Worth noting alongside: `FloatingPointModel=Fast` is set in all four ArtAttack configurations (ArtAttack.vcxproj:94, 116, 139, 165), which licenses the compiler to assume operands are not NaN — so if a caller is ever added, the failure will be silent rather than loud.

One method holds the application's entire flow graph: `switch (_state)` with a nested `switch (action)` inside three of its cases, spanning :128-301. Within it the same five-line block is pasted five times (:206-213, :246-254, :256-265, :266-275, :284-293), differing only in one enum value:
```cpp
this->_level->stop_music();
MainMenuMidwayLoadSettings settings;
settings.settings = <this->_settings | MenuLevelSettings()>;
settings.screen   = main_menu_screen::<HOME|TEAM_SELECT|WEAPON_SELECT|STAGE_SELECT>;
this->get_context()->transition_to(std::make_unique<GameMenu>(this->get_data(), settings));
