At namespace scope `const` implies internal linkage, so every TU including TeamColour.h gets its own private copy. TeamColour.h is included by Level.h, LevelBuilder.h, LevelEndInfo.h, PaintTile.h, PlayerBuilder.h and StructurePaintable.h — most of the simulation.

Because `MattMath::Colour` has user-provided, non-constexpr constructors (MattMath.h:593-600, including `Colour(int,int,int,int)` and `Colour(const std::string&)`), it is not a literal type, so these are **dynamically** initialised at static-init time, once per TU. It currently works only because Colour.h is included first (TeamColour.h:5), giving the right within-TU ordering; nothing enforces that.

This header drags in `Colour.h`, which is the same pattern at scale: ~150 `const MattMath::Colour` at namespace scope plus a `static MattMath::Colour colour_from_name(const std::string&)` function defined in the header (Colour.h:315+), giving every TU its own copy of both.

`virtual bool get_for_deletion() const = 0;` — an object can only be *asked* whether it wants to die. There is no interface to tell one to die and no reason attached. `Level` polls it and does a hand-written swap-with-back erase using a decrementing `size_t` index (Level.cpp:296-305):
```cpp
for (size_t i = 0; i < this->_collision_objects->size(); i++)
    if (this->_collision_objects->at(i)->get_for_deletion()) {
        this->_collision_objects->at(i) = std::move(this->_collision_objects->back());
        this->_collision_objects->pop_back();
        i--;   // wraps to SIZE_MAX when i == 0, then ++ brings it back to 0
    }
```
Meanwhile other lifetime conditions in the same function are signalled by throwing instead (Level.cpp:312, 319, `"Player out of bounds"`). So object lifetime is split across a per-object boolean, one hand-rolled erase loop hardcoded to one specific container, and exceptions — three mechanisms for one concern, none of them owned by anything.

The name is also wrong for a bool predicate: `get_for_deletion()` should be `is_marked_for_deletion()`.

```cpp
int GameLevel::check_for_pause_input(const std::vector<PlayerInputData>& player_inputs)
{
    for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(player_inputs.size()); i++)
        if (player_inputs[i].toggle_pause_menu) return i;
    return -1;
}
```
The caller must know that -1 is the magic "nobody" value and compares against a bare literal (`if (pause_menu_player != -1)`, :146). The `static_cast<int>` on `size()` exists purely to make the sentinel expressible in the loop's signed index. And the codebase contains **zero** uses of any `<algorithm>` header — this is a textbook `std::find_if`.

This is the design half of the critical input-indexing finding: the returned value is also semantically wrong (an index into a compacted vector used as a player number). Fixing the sentinel without fixing the indexing leaves the real bug in place.

