    ...
    i++;
}
```

Three problems in seven lines:
1. `i` counts *all* players, not players on that team. With players [A, B, A, B] team A uses `team_a_spawns[0]` and `[2]` while `team_a_spawns[1]` is never used — spawn point assignment is effectively arbitrary and the level author cannot reason about it.
2. `std::vector::operator[]` with no bounds check against `.size()`. The three shipping levels each declare exactly 4 spawns per team (verified in king_of_the_hill.json, turbulence.json, close_quarters.json) and the menu caps at 4 players, so it doesn't overflow *today* — but any level authored with 2 spawns, or any future 8-player mode, is a silent out-of-bounds read with no diagnostic. The level format has no way to declare its supported player count.
3. `MenuPlayerSettings::team` defaults to `player_team::NONE` (MenuPlayerSettings.h:9). The `else` branch swallows NONE and gives it a team-B spawn rather than rejecting it.

```cpp
void ResourceLoader::load_textures() const { this->load_sprite_sheet_from_directory(TEXTURE_DIRECTORY, "sprite_sheet_1"); }
void ResourceLoader::load_fonts() { ... "gill_sans_ultra_bold_144" ... "courier_new_bold_16"; }
void ResourceLoader::load_level_info() const {
    this->load_level_info_from_directory(LEVEL_DIRECTORY, "king_of_the_hill", level_stage::KING_OF_THE_HILL);
    this->load_level_info_from_directory(LEVEL_DIRECTORY, "turbulence", level_stage::TURBULENCE);
    this->load_level_info_from_directory(LEVEL_DIRECTORY, "close_quarters", level_stage::CLOSE_QUARTERS);
}
```

There is no manifest file anywhere in the repo. The complete list of this game's assets lives in these 35 lines of the engine's resource loader. Compounding it, `ResourceManager` keys its level cache on the *game's* enum: `std::map<level_stage, std::unique_ptr<LevelLoadedInfo>> _level_infos;` with `level_stage` values `KING_OF_THE_HILL`, `TURBULENCE`, `CLOSE_QUARTERS` (ResourceManager.h:7,32,34,52; level_stage.h:4-11).

For a project whose stated goal is a reusable 2D engine, this means: a second game cannot use `ResourceLoader`/`ResourceManager` at all without editing them, and adding one level to *this* game requires touching level_stage.h, ResourceLoader.cpp and MainMenu.cpp (which switches on the enum at :1870 and computes `rand() % MAX_STAGE` at :1893-1895).

```cpp
const static std::string TEXTURE_DIRECTORY = "./textures/";
const static std::string LEVEL_DIRECTORY   = "./levels/";
const static std::string FONT_DIRECTORY    = "./fonts/";
const static std::string SAVE_FILE_PATH    = "./save/save_data.json";
const static std::string SOUNDS_DIRECTORY  = "./sounds/";
```

These resolve against the process working directory. `ArtAttack.vcxproj` has no `PostBuildEvent`, no `<Content Include>` items, no `CopyFileToFolders`, and no `<LocalDebuggerWorkingDirectory>` (verified by grep). The only reason the game runs is that the VS debugger defaults the working directory to `$(ProjectDir)`, which happens to be where `textures/`, `levels/`, `fonts/` and `sounds/` live. Double-clicking the built exe, or launching it from anywhere else, finds nothing — and per the finding above, finds nothing by crashing on a null FILE*.

Secondary: `const static std::string` at namespace scope in a header gives internal linkage — a separate copy plus dynamic initialisation of five `std::string`s in each of the 81 translation units. There is also no way to redirect the data root at runtime (dev vs. installed layout, per-user save location, packaged archive).

```cpp
static MattMath::Colour colour_from_name(const std::string& name) {
    if (name == "ALICE_BLUE") return colour_consts::ALICE_BLUE;
    ... 148 more ...
    return colour_consts::WHITE;   // :465
}
```

This is the sole validation point for the `"colour"` field on all 91 objects in the three level JSONs (LevelObjectBuilder.cpp:65, 142, 176, 196). A typo — `"DARK_GERY"`, or a colour name that exists in the HTML set but not in the if-chain — silently produces white geometry with no error, which is indistinguishable from an intentionally white object.

The implementation is also expensive and duplicated: `static` at namespace scope in a header gives it internal linkage, so all 150 string comparisons are compiled into each of the 81 TUs that include Colour.h, alongside the 153 namespace-scope `const MattMath::Colour` objects and 149 `const std::string`s in the same header, each of which gets a per-TU copy with dynamic initialisation.

The level data uses a small fraction of these; a grep of the level JSONs would confirm well under 20 distinct colour names are actually referenced.

`load_sprite_font` (:83-101) is the sole loader with a try/catch:
```cpp
catch (const std::exception&) {
    std::string error = "SpriteFont " + font_name + " not found at " + font_path + ".";
    throw std::out_of_range(error);
}
```
It catches *every* exception — corrupt file, out of video memory, device removed — and reports all of them as "not found", discarding the original `what()`. `load_texture`, `load_sprite_sheet`, `load_level_info` and `load_sound_bank` have no handling at all, so `DX::ThrowIfFailed` (:60) and the `WaveBank` constructor (:156) propagate raw HRESULT exceptions out through `Game::initialize`.

Separately, three sites widen a narrow path by copying bytes into wchar_t: `std::wstring(texture_path.begin(), texture_path.end())` at :62, the same for fonts at :91-92 and for the wave bank at :158-159. This is only correct for ASCII; the moment a path picks up a non-ASCII character (an install directory, a user name) it produces mojibake and the file is not found.

Minor: `load_texture` at :66-70 does `resource.As(&texture)` and `texture->GetDesc(&texture_desc)`, then discards `texture_desc` entirely — a pointless second COM QueryInterface plus driver call on every texture load.

Also structural: the ten `load_X` / `load_X_from_directory` pairs (:54-175) each duplicate `directory + name + ".ext"` with the extension hardcoded per asset type.

`PlayerInput::update_and_get_player_inputs()` (ArtAttack/PlayerInput.cpp:126-137) builds its result with `if (current[i].connected) { ... result.push_back(input); }` — the vector is COMPACTED, so its indices are "nth connected pad", not "player number". `Level::update_level_logic` then does:

```
int player_index = 0;
for (const auto& object : *this->_player_objects)
{
    object->set_player_input(player_inputs[player_index]);
```

`_player_objects` is fixed at level-build time. The moment any pad disconnects, `player_inputs.size() < _player_objects->size()` and `operator[]` reads past the end — and the players that survive are driven by the WRONG controller (unplug pad 0 in a 2-player match and player 0 is now steered by pad 1). If *all* pads go away the vector is empty and `player_inputs[0]` is an out-of-bounds read on an empty vector on the very first iteration.

That last case is not hypothetical and is reachable without touching any hardware: minimising the window sends WM_SIZE/SIZE_MINIMIZED (ArtAttack/Main.cpp:281-291) → `Game::on_suspending()` (ArtAttack/Game.cpp:171-176) → `_gamepad->Suspend()`, while the main loop keeps calling `tick()` (ArtAttack/Main.cpp:213-224) → `GameLevel::update()` ACTIVE branch → `_player_input->update_and_get_player_inputs()` on a suspended GamePad, which reports every pad disconnected. So minimising during a match walks straight into the empty-vector index.

The same index-confusion exists in the pause menu: `GameLevel::check_for_pause_input` (ArtAttack/GameStates.cpp:348-358) returns `i`, an index into the compacted `player_inputs`, which is stored as `player_num` and later used as a direct subscript into a *different* compacted vector — `inputs[player_num]` at ArtAttack/PauseMenu.cpp:48-53 and 217-222. Note that every other menu iterates `for (i = 0; i < inputs.size(); i++)` correctly (ArtAttack/MainMenu.cpp:49-51, ArtAttack/EndMenu.cpp:32, ArtAttack/ResultsMenu.cpp:266) — only the gameplay and pause paths get it wrong.

```
switch (this->_state)
{
case game_level_state::FIRST_UPDATE:
    this->_state = game_level_state::SECOND_UPDATE;
    return;
case game_level_state::SECOND_UPDATE:
    this->_state = game_level_state::ACTIVE;
    //no break here so it goes to active
```

Two extra enumerators were bolted onto the level state machine (ArtAttack/GameStates.h:38-39) purely to swallow update calls after a load. It absorbs exactly ONE update (FIRST returns; SECOND falls through and immediately simulates), but the missing `ResetElapsedTime()` produces up to SIX. So five full simulation steps still run unseen. The mechanism treats a symptom whose cause is one missing line.

It also produces a refresh-rate-dependent black flash. `Game::render()` (ArtAttack/Game.cpp:103-121) runs unconditionally every tick and always does `clear()` + `Present()`, but `GameLevel::draw()` (GameStates.cpp:305-309) returns before drawing anything while in FIRST_UPDATE/SECOND_UPDATE. On a 60 Hz vsync display that is ~2 black frames on level entry. On a 144 Hz display `update()` runs on only ~42% of ticks, so the same two state transitions take ~5-6 presented frames — the black flash is over twice as long purely because the monitor is faster.

```
void Game::tick()
{
    _timer.Tick([&]() { update(_timer); });
    render();
}
```

`Tick` invokes the lambda 0..6 times per call (ArtAttack/StepTimer.h:134-142), and `update()` → `StateContext::update()` is the *only* thing that ever touches the pad. Every poll site is downstream of it: `GameLevel::update` ACTIVE branch calls `_player_input->update_and_get_player_inputs()` (ArtAttack/GameStates.cpp:141-142), and every menu page calls `MenuPage::get_menu_inputs()` → `get_input()->update_and_get_menu_inputs()` (ArtAttack/MenuPage.cpp:185-188). Each of those loops `for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) get_raw_input(i)` → `_gamepad->GetState(i, ...)` (ArtAttack/PlayerInput.cpp:119-125, ArtAttack/MenuInput.cpp:131-137), so one update = 4 XInput/WGI reads, and one displayed frame = 0, 4 or up to 24 reads.

Consequences:
- The pad is sampled at exactly 60 Hz no matter the display. All edge detection is a two-sample comparison — `jump_pressed = current.jump_button && !previous.jump_button` (PlayerInput.cpp:45-48), `pause_menu = !current.pause_menu && previous.pause_menu` (:53), `proceed = prev_proceed && no_press_current` (MenuInput.cpp:40-46). A press-and-release that fits inside one 16.7 ms window is invisible to both classes — it never appears in `previous`, so the whole press is dropped. There is no latching anywhere.
- On a 120/144 Hz display `update()` runs 0 times on the majority of ticks, adding up to a full 16.7 ms of input latency on top of the pipeline, while `render()` still runs.
- During catch-up the same physical pad state is read 6 times in under a millisecond, so 100 ms of simulated gameplay is driven by one instant of input.

```
void ResultsMenuInitial::update()
{
    const float dt = *this->get_data()->get_dt();
    std::vector<ProcessedMenuInput> menu_inputs = this->get_menu_inputs();
    if (this->_delay_timer < RESULTS_MENU_TEAM_FILL_DELAY) { this->_delay_timer += dt; }
    else { ... if (this->_fill_timer >= RESULTS_MENU_TEAM_FILL_TIME) { ... if (this->_show_results_timer >= RESULTS_MENU_SHOW_RESULTS_DELAY) { ... int continue_input = this->check_for_continue_input(menu_inputs);
```

The poll at line 32 always runs — which correctly advances `MenuInput::_prev_inputs` — but the computed PROCEED edge is only *read* at line 58, inside two nested timer gates. Because the edge is derived from a single-frame comparison and is not latched anywhere, a release-edge that lands on the frame before `_show_results_timer` crosses the threshold is destroyed: `_prev_inputs` has already been overwritten, so the same press cannot be seen again. A player mashing A through the fill animation gets one specific press silently eaten and has to press again.

This is the general shape of the problem — edges are consumed by whoever happens to poll, whether or not they act on them — and it is unavoidable while edge state lives inside the poller.

```
this->_time_elapsed += *this->_dt;
float frame_time = this->_frame_time;
if (this->_time_elapsed > frame_time)
{
    this->_frame_index++;
    ...
    this->_time_elapsed -= frame_time;
}
```

It is an `if`, not a `while`. If `*_dt` ever exceeds `frame_time`, `_time_elapsed` grows without bound while the animation advances at most one frame per update — animations play in slow motion and drift permanently out of sync with the simulation. The identical code exists in `AnimatedSprite::update` (ArtAttack/AnimatedSprite.cpp:59-84, copy-pasted).

This is latent today only because `*_dt` happens to be the constant 0.0166666 (fixed timestep) and the shipped animation strips have longer frame times. It becomes a live bug the instant anyone sets `SetFixedTimeStep(false)` (the DirectXTK default, and what the template comment at ArtAttack/Game.cpp:62-63 invites), lowers `TARGET_FPS`, or authors a fast strip. For an engine that is supposed to be reusable, an animation system that only works for one hard-coded step size is a defect regardless of whether it currently misbehaves.

`Level::draw_zoom_out_level_component` permanently mutates the shared ViewportManager at the end of every match:

    // Level.cpp:619 (inside a `const` draw helper, run on a thread-pool worker)
    this->_viewport_manager->set_layout(screen_layout::ONE_PLAYER);

Grep confirms `ViewportManager::set_layout` has exactly two call sites: `GameStates.cpp:123` (`GameLevel::init`) and `Level.cpp:619`. The end-menu RESTART branch rebuilds the level but never restores the layout:

