    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++)
        if (current[i].connected) result.push_back(...);

So the index is not a gamepad number, and the two vectors are sampled on different frames. If a pad disconnects between the frame the pause was detected and the next frame's `PauseMenuInitial::update`, `inputs.size()` shrinks and `inputs[player_num]` is an unchecked out-of-bounds read on a heap vector. The same index is also used as a *display* number (`get_player_number_text`, PauseMenu.cpp:135-136), so with pad 0 unplugged, physical player 2 pausing is labelled "Pause Menu P1".

The codebase already knows about this: every main-menu screen guards with `for (int i = 0; i < num_inputs && i < player_count; i++)` (MainMenu.cpp:1160, 1443, 1790). The pause menu — the only screen that indexes rather than iterates — does not. The same latent mismatch exists at `Level.cpp:192` (`object->set_player_input(player_inputs[player_index])`, indexed by position in `_player_objects`).

    void StateContext::transition_to(std::unique_ptr<State> state)
    {
        this->_state = std::move(state);   // destroys the OLD state -- which is the caller
        this->_state->set_context(this);
        this->_state->init();
    }

Every call is made from inside the outgoing state's own `update()`: GameStates.cpp:81-84 (`GameMenu::order_level_creation`), 211-212, 252-253, 262-263, 272-273, 290-291 (all five end-menu/pause exits), PauseMenu.cpp:70-72, 77-79, 225-226, 250-252, MainMenu.cpp:1505-1506, 1797-1799. In every case `this` is freed while `this->update()` is still on the stack; execution then continues through `break;`/`return;` in the freed object's member function.

It survives only by luck — no member is touched after the call. `PauseMenuInitial::update` (PauseMenu.cpp:70-73) does not even `return` after transitioning; it relies on the subsequent `else if` chain being skipped. Adding one line of cleanup after any of these calls is an immediate use-after-free.

The secondary effect is that `GameLevel::init()` — a full level build: JSON parse, texture/sound lookup, object construction (LevelBuilder.cpp:24-83) — runs *inside* `transition_to`, i.e. inside the destroyed `GameMenu`'s stack frame, adding a multi-hundred-millisecond hitch to that frame with no loading screen.

findings.suggestion:
Make the transition deferred and non-reentrant:

    void StateContext::transition_to(std::unique_ptr<State> next) { _pending = std::move(next); }
    void StateContext::update() {
        if (_state) _state->update();
        if (_pending) { _state = std::move(_pending); _state->set_context(this); _state->init(); }
    }

That keeps the outgoing state alive for the whole of its own `update()`, makes the self-delete impossible, and gives you an obvious place to hang an async/loading-screen build later. Also note `StateContext::update()`/`draw()` are declared `const` (StateContext.h:12-13) while driving all mutation in the game — that const should go.

findings.detail:
    ID3D11ShaderResourceView* ResourceManager::get_texture(const std::string& texture_name)
    {
        try { return this->_textures[texture_name].Get(); }
        catch (const std::out_of_range&) { throw std::out_of_range("Texture " + texture_name + " not found."); }
    }

`std::map::operator[]` never throws `out_of_range` — it default-inserts. So a misspelled or missing name silently inserts a null entry and returns `nullptr`, and the caller null-dereferences somewhere far away instead of getting the intended diagnostic. The catch block is unreachable dead code. Identical at `get_sprite_font` (line 31), `get_sprite_sheet` (line 51), `get_sound_bank` (line 95). Only `get_level_info` (line 71) uses `.at()` correctly.

This is squarely on the end-of-match path: `SoundBankObject::get_sb()` (SoundBankObject.cpp:16) calls `get_sound_bank` on **every** sound call — `EndMenuInitial::update` alone hits it at EndMenu.cpp:38, 45, 52, 59, 66, 74, 103 — and `EndMenuPage`'s bank name is a bare string literal (`end_menu_consts::SOUND_BANK`, EndMenu.h:36).

A side effect is that all four getters are non-`const` mutating operations on a shared map, which is why `ResourceManager*` can never be handed to the thread-pool draw tasks safely (`MenuPage::draw_mobjects_in_viewports`, MenuPage.cpp:140-150, and `Level::draw_player_view_level`, Level.cpp:491-593, both call into them from workers).

