Both input pumps drop disconnected slots instead of reporting them:

```cpp
std::vector<PlayerInputData> result;
for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++)
{
    if (current[i].connected)
    {
        result.push_back(calculate_player_input(current[i], this->_prev_inputs[i]));  // PlayerInput.cpp:131-133
    }
    this->_prev_inputs[i] = current[i];
}
return result;
```

Identical shape in `MenuInput::update_and_get_menu_inputs`, MenuInput.cpp:138-149.

The returned index is therefore a *rank among currently-connected pads*, not a pad identity, and it silently renumbers whenever the set of connected pads changes. Meanwhile `PlayerInputData::connection` (PlayerInputData.h:19) and `ProcessedMenuInput::connection` (MenuInput.h:41) default to `DISCONNECTED` and are hardcoded to `CONNECTED` at PlayerInput.cpp:111 and MenuInput.cpp:127 - because they are only ever set on the path that already knows the pad is connected. `connection_state` (connection_state.h:4-8) is written but never read anywhere in the project (grep confirms zero consumers). The mechanism designed to solve this exact problem is present and inert.

Re-plug into a different slot: unplug the pad in slot 0 during a 2-player match and plug it into slot 2. Before: slots {0,1} -> indices {0,1}. After: slots {1,2} -> indices {0,1}. The pad that was player 1 is now index 1 and drives player 2's character; the pad that was player 2 is now index 0 and drives player 1's. Both players' controls silently swap, with no event, no message, and no way to detect it - `Player::_player_num` (Player.cpp:1077) is assigned from the menu (`set_player_num(i, i)`, MainMenu.cpp:1233) and never correlated with a hardware slot.

This is the single root cause of the Level.cpp:192 and PauseMenu.cpp:53 out-of-bounds reads and of the team-select deadlock.

Confirmed. `MainMenuTeamSelect::update` bounds its input loop by the smaller of connected pads and player count:

```cpp
int num_inputs = static_cast<int>(inputs.size());
int player_count = this->get_player_count();
for (int i = 0; i < num_inputs && i < player_count; i++)   // MainMenu.cpp:1160
{ ... this->_select_states[i].state = confirmation_state::CONFIRMED; ... }
```

but the exit condition scans all `player_count` slots:

```cpp
bool MainMenuTeamSelect::all_players_confirmed() const
{
    for (int i = 0; i < this->get_player_count(); i++)         // MainMenu.cpp:1238
    {
        if (this->_select_states[i].state != confirmation_state::CONFIRMED)
            return false;
    }
    return true;
}
```

`_select_states` is sized to `get_player_count()` in `init()` (MainMenu.cpp:1345-1351) and every entry starts `UNCONFIRMED` (line 1348). Indices `>= num_inputs` are unreachable by the loop at 1160, so `all_players_confirmed()` at MainMenu.cpp:1215 can never return true and the transition to weapon select never fires.

The exact blocking condition is `num_inputs < player_count`, where `num_inputs` is the number of connected pads at that frame. There is nothing on screen telling the user this: the page renders `player_count` rows of team widgets (MainMenu.cpp:1345-1400) and the rows for absent pads just sit inert on "center/NONE".

Because `MainMenuPlayerCount` (MainMenu.cpp:880-1008) offers 1-4 players unconditionally with no reference to how many pads are present, the trap is one button press away from the previous screen. Choosing 2 players with 1 pad is a dead end.

It is escapable, not a hard lock: B on the connected pad hits `all_players_unconfirmed()` (MainMenu.cpp:1164) and backs out, though it takes two presses if player 1 already confirmed.

`MainMenuWeaponSelect` has the identical shape (loop guard at MainMenu.cpp:1443, `all_players_confirmed()` at 1555-1565) and is reachable directly from player count in the 1-player case, so it deadlocks the same way if that single pad drops. `MainMenuStageSelect` (MainMenu.cpp:1790) escapes this because it uses one shared `_select_state` rather than per-player state.

A related consequence of the compacted-vector design: if the pad set changes between the player-count page and team select, `inputs[i]` addresses a different physical pad than it did a frame earlier, so a player can find themselves driving another player's team widget.

The three handlers sit next to each other and only one of them checks:

```cpp
void Game::on_activated() const
{
    if (this->_gamepad)          // Game.cpp:161 - guarded
        this->_gamepad->Resume();
}

void Game::on_deactivated() const
{
    this->_gamepad->Suspend();   // Game.cpp:168 - NOT guarded
}

void Game::on_suspending() const
{
    this->_gamepad->Suspend();   // Game.cpp:174 - NOT guarded
    _audio_engine->Suspend();    // Game.cpp:175 - NOT guarded
}
```

`_gamepad` and `_audio_engine` are `= nullptr` members (Game.h:68, Game.h:74) created only inside `Game::initialize` (Game.cpp:59 and Game.cpp:47). Main.cpp calls `initialize` at line 207 - *after* `CreateWindowExW` (Main.cpp:156/162) and `ShowWindow` (Main.cpp:191/195), both of which synchronously dispatch `WM_ACTIVATEAPP` into `WndProc`. `GWLP_USERDATA` is already populated by `WM_CREATE` (Main.cpp:246-252), so the `if (game)` check at Main.cpp:330 passes and the only thing between the message and a null dereference is the guard that `on_deactivated` lacks.

So launching the game while another window steals focus (start from a script, click away during the several-second `load_all_resources()` at Game.cpp:258, or launch minimised) routes `WM_ACTIVATEAPP` wParam==0 -> `on_deactivated()` -> `nullptr->Suspend()`. The presence of the guard on `on_activated` shows the author already hit this on the activate path and patched only that one.

The same asymmetry means `on_suspending` (reached via `WM_SIZE`/`SIZE_MINIMIZED`, Main.cpp:282-291, and `PBT_APMQUERYSUSPEND`, Main.cpp:346-350) is a double null-deref during the startup window.

```cpp
MSG msg = {};
while (WM_QUIT != msg.message)
{
    if (PeekMessage(&msg, nullptr, 0, 0, PM_REMOVE)) { TranslateMessage(&msg); DispatchMessage(&msg); }
    else { g_game->tick(); }          // Main.cpp:222 - unconditional
}
```

`WndProc` maintains `s_in_suspend` and `s_minimized` (Main.cpp:236-238) and calls `game->on_suspending()` / `on_resuming()`, but neither flag is ever consulted by the loop and `Game` keeps no equivalent. Consequences:

- While minimised, `Game::tick` (Game.cpp:75-84) still runs the fixed-timestep update and `render()` -> `_device_resources->Present()` every iteration, burning CPU/GPU on an invisible window.
- `Game::update` calls `_audio_engine->Update()` at Game.cpp:93 on an engine that `on_suspending` just Suspended at Game.cpp:175.
- Most importantly for input: `Game::on_deactivated` calls `_gamepad->Suspend()` (Game.cpp:168) on every alt-tab, and the state machine keeps polling it. If a match or the pause menu is on screen at that moment, `PlayerInput::update_and_get_player_inputs` / `MenuInput::update_and_get_menu_inputs` return a shorter or empty vector while `Level::update_level_logic` (Level.cpp:192) and `PauseMenuInitial::update` (PauseMenu.cpp:53) keep indexing by player ordinal - the two out-of-bounds reads reported separately, now reachable by an ordinary alt-tab rather than by unplugging hardware.

Held-button state across an alt-tab is actually handled correctly by accident: `_prev_inputs[i] = current[i]` at PlayerInput.cpp:135 and MenuInput.cpp:147 is outside the `connected` check, so a slot that reads disconnected resets its previous state to all-false and no spurious press/release edge fires on return. That part is fine; the unconditional tick is not.

Two problems in one construct, twice.

First, the branches cannot execute. At MenuInput.cpp:48-70:

```cpp
if (proceed)      { result.action = PROCEED; }
else if (back)    { result.action = BACK; }
else if (pause)   { result.action = PAUSE; }
else if ((proceed && back) || (proceed && pause) || (pause && back))   // line 60 - unreachable
{
    throw new std::exception("2 or more of proceed, back or pause pressed at same time");   // line 64
}
```

Reaching line 60 requires `!proceed && !back && !pause`, which makes every disjunct false. The check is also redundant by construction: `prev_proceed` already requires `!previous.back && !previous.pause` (lines 41-43), so the three flags are mutually exclusive before the chain begins. Identical dead branch for the direction chain at MenuInput.cpp:118-122 (`else if (up && down && left && right)` after `if (up) / else if (down) / else if (left) / else if (right)`).

Second, and worse if either branch were ever reachable: `throw new std::exception(...)` throws a `std::exception*`, not a `std::exception`. No `catch (const std::exception&)` anywhere would match it, so it would propagate to `std::terminate`, and the heap allocation would leak. These are the only two `throw new` sites in the project - every other throw site (58 of them) uses `throw std::exception("...")` by value.

As a bonus, `std::exception(const char*)` is an MSVC-only extension, so none of this is portable off the Microsoft STL.

```cpp
std::vector<ProcessedMenuInput> MenuPage::get_menu_inputs() const   // declared const, MenuPage.h:53
{
    return this->get_data()->get_input()->update_and_get_menu_inputs();
}
```

`update_and_get_menu_inputs` advances `MenuInput::_prev_inputs` (MenuInput.cpp:147), and every action in the system is edge-detected against that previous frame (`proceed = prev_proceed && no_press_current`, MenuInput.cpp:44). So calling this "getter" *consumes* the edge: a second call in the same frame returns all-NONE, and the caller that would have handled the press silently gets nothing.

The `const` qualifier actively misleads - it advertises that the call is side-effect-free when it mutates the global input state for the whole frame. It compiles only because the mutation happens through a raw `MenuInput*` (`MenuData::get_input()`, MenuData.cpp:16), which const does not propagate through.

Today every page happens to call it exactly once at the top of `update()` (MainMenu.cpp:49, 133, 302, 647, 882, 1156, 1439, 1786; PauseMenu.cpp:48, 217; EndMenu.cpp:28), so the bug is latent rather than live. But it is one refactor away: any page that adds a helper needing input, or any nested `StateContext` that updates two pages in a frame, will silently drop presses with no diagnostic.

Same shape on the gameplay side - `PlayerInput::update_and_get_player_inputs()` is called once per frame at GameStates.cpp:142 and its result threaded manually to both `check_for_pause_input` (line 145) and `Level::update` (line 185), because a second call would eat the pause edge.

Two of the four `SpriteSheet::draw` overloads are non-const and index the frame map with `operator[]`:

```cpp
void SpriteSheet::draw(SpriteBatch* sprite_batch, const std::string& frame_name, ...)  // :92, no trailing const
{
    sprite_batch->Draw(this->_texture, position.get_xm_vector(),
        this->_sprite_frames[frame_name].get_source_rectangle(),   // :105
        ...);
}
// identical at :114-132, line :126
```

`_sprite_frames` is `std::map<std::string, SpriteFrame>` (SpriteSheet.h:61) and `std::map::operator[]` **default-constructs and inserts** when the key is absent — a tree mutation. `SpriteSheet` objects are owned once by `ResourceManager` and handed out as raw pointers to every `SpriteSheetObject`, so a single sheet is shared by every player, projectile and tile.

Those objects' `draw()` calls all funnel into this from inside the thread-pool tasks submitted at Level.cpp:388 and MenuPage.cpp:142. Concurrent `operator[]` on one `std::map` from N threads is undefined behaviour even when every key already exists (it is a non-const member function on shared state), and a genuine red-black-tree corruption the first time any lookup misses — e.g. a typo'd frame name in a JSON sheet, which today silently inserts an empty `SpriteFrame` instead of failing.

Note the other two overloads (:134, :156) are correctly `const` and take a pre-resolved `RECT*`, which proves the const version is achievable.

```cpp
class ThreadPool {
public:
    void add_task(std::function<void()> task);
    void wait_for_tasks_to_complete();   // drains EVERYTHING
private:
    std::vector<PTP_WORK> _work_items;   // :27  no mutex, no documentation of affinity
};
```

There is exactly one `ThreadPool` in the process (Game.cpp:236), published as a raw pointer through `GameData` (GameData.h:60) and used by two unrelated subsystems: `Level::draw_active_level`/`draw_zoom_out_level` (Level.cpp:388, 601) and `MenuPage::draw_mobjects_in_viewports` (MenuPage.cpp:142).

I traced every caller: `add_task` appears at exactly those three sites, and none of the three task bodies (`draw_player_view_level`, `draw_zoom_out_level_component`, `draw_range_of_mobjects_in_viewports`) calls `add_task` — so today `_work_items` is only ever touched by the main thread and the unsynchronised vector is not *currently* racing. But nothing in the type says so, and two properties make that safety accidental rather than designed:

1. `wait_for_tasks_to_complete()` drains and clears the entire vector. It has no idea which caller submitted what. The moment two owners have work in flight (nested draw, a task that spawns work, a second pool user added later), one owner's wait will `CloseThreadpoolWork` handles the other owner is still using — undefined behaviour with no diagnostic.
2. `GameLevel::draw` (GameStates.cpp:315-343) already interleaves the two users within one frame: `_level->draw(...)` submits and drains, then `_pause_menu->draw()` submits and drains against the *same* vector. It works only because the first drain completes before the second begins.

For a codebase whose stated goal is a reusable engine, an untyped, unowned, single-batch, thread-affine-by-accident pool is the wrong primitive to hand to arbitrary subsystems.

```cpp
ThreadPool::~ThreadPool()
{
    CloseThreadpoolCleanupGroupMembers(_cleanup_group, FALSE, nullptr);   // :32
    CloseThreadpoolCleanupGroup(_cleanup_group);
    CloseThreadpool(_pool);
    DestroyThreadpoolEnvironment(&_callback_environment);
}
```

`fCancelPendingCallbacks = FALSE` means *do not cancel* — the call blocks until every submitted callback, including ones that have not started, has run to completion. Two consequences:

1. The destructor is a hidden unbounded block on whatever thread destroys the pool. Since the pool is destroyed from `create_device_dependent_resources` (Game.cpp:236, reached via `OnDeviceRestored`) and from `~Game` (Main.cpp:226), that is always the main/UI thread. There is no comment saying so, and no `wait_for_tasks_to_complete()` call before either destruction — the blocking behaviour of this one line is the *only* thing standing between the codebase and a use-after-free on a worker.

2. `FALSE` is load-bearing for a reason the code does not state: the closure is a raw `new std::function<void()>` (ThreadPool.cpp:41) whose only `delete` is inside the callback body (ThreadPool.cpp:64). Change that flag to `TRUE` — the natural thing for anyone trying to make shutdown responsive — and every cancelled work item leaks its closure with no way to recover it, because `SetThreadpoolCallbackCleanupGroup(&_callback_environment, _cleanup_group, nullptr)` (ThreadPool.cpp:27) passes `nullptr` for the cleanup-group cancel callback, which is the exact hook that exists to free per-work-item state on cancellation.

Also note `_work_items` is not cleared or closed here; if a draw ever unwound without waiting, the vector's stale handles are released by the cleanup group as a side effect, which happens to be correct but is not obvious and is not what the code appears to intend.

```cpp
void StateContext::transition_to(std::unique_ptr<State> state)
{
    this->_state = std::move(state);   // :22  destroys the CURRENTLY EXECUTING state
    this->_state->set_context(this);
    this->_state->init();
}
```

Every caller reaches this from inside the outgoing state's own `update()`:
- `GameMenu::order_level_creation` (GameStates.cpp:79-84), called from `GameMenu::update` line 76;
- `GameLevel::update`, pause-menu QUIT (GameStates.cpp:211-212);
- `GameLevel::update`, end-menu CHANGE_TEAMS / CHANGE_WEAPONS / CHANGE_LEVEL / EXIT (GameStates.cpp:252, 262, 272, 290).

At line 22 the `unique_ptr` assignment deletes the old `State`, so `this` inside `GameMenu::update` / `GameLevel::update` is freed while those frames are still on the stack, as is `StateContext::update`'s `this->_state->update()` call at StateContext.cpp:10. It survives today only because every one of those six sites happens to touch no member after the call — `order_level_creation` returns immediately, and the five `GameLevel` cases fall straight through `break; break;` to the end of the function. That is one added line away from a use-after-free, in six places, with nothing marking the hazard.

It also interacts badly with the rest of this review: the transition destroys `GameLevel`, and with it the `Level` whose `this` is captured by every draw task (Level.cpp:388, 601). Today the ordering saves it — `Game::update` runs all transitions, `Game::render` runs afterwards, and each draw drains the pool before returning (Game.cpp:75-84, 91, 115) — so a transition can never land while tasks are outstanding. But that is an emergent property of three unrelated pieces of code, not a stated invariant; the moment any draw returns without draining (see the `add_task` exception finding), a transition on the next frame frees the `Level` that live callbacks are still reading.
